Title: What They Called Recovery
Credit: Animated feature screenplay — whole-film review draft v0.1
Author: Christopher Kokkinos
Draft date: 2026-10-10
Status: All 57 working scenes developed; independent completion under DEC-0019; author refinements during production; dispositions remain provisional

# SEQ-01 — THE DRIVER AND THE ASSIGNMENT

= SC001 — Elias's apartment (accepted direction, DEC-0012)

INT. ELIAS'S FLAT - BEDROOM/KITCHEN - MORNING

Two PIGEONS stamp along the narrow window ledge. One pecks at the glass.

ELIAS, still in yesterday's clothes, opens his eyes. A clock beside a fake fireplace reads eight. The alarm switch is OFF.

He sits up. Pain catches beneath his left ribs. He steadies himself with his right arm; his shoulder objects too.

The bedside drawer is open a finger's width. Inside: a prescription blister strip of OXYCODONE. Two tablets are gone. Elias looks at it, then pushes the drawer shut.

Across the flat, the kitchen surfaces are clean. His clothes and unopened post are less orderly. A torch and a small set of tools by the door are precisely arranged.

He starts the shower. Hot water lasts seconds.

ELIAS
Excellent.

He dresses, makes coffee, and picks up a white envelope from the kitchen table. MAC is written on the front.

He opens it once more. The short notice inside reads: "I will complete work already accepted. I will not take further assignments after Friday. Please arrange final payment."

Elias folds the letter and puts it in the inside pocket of his jacket. He pauses with his hand there, then takes his keys.

At the window, the pigeons watch him.

ELIAS
Flat's yours.

He goes out. A pigeon pecks the glass again.

= SC002 — Mrs Doran (draft)

INT. ELIAS'S BUILDING - STAIRWELL/LOBBY - MORNING

The lift indicator holds at FIFTEEN. Elias presses the button. Nothing. He takes the stairs, coffee in hand.

On the third-floor landing, MRS DORAN struggles with a door that will not close. A wheeled shopping trolley waits beside her.

MRS DORAN
You're late.

ELIAS
You've been speaking to Mac.

MRS DORAN
I've been hearing you run down these stairs for nineteen years.

ELIAS
Twenty.

MRS DORAN
Then you've been late longer than I thought.

She pulls the door again. It jams halfway. Elias sets down his coffee, lifts the door by the handle and pushes it inward. The lower hinge settles. The lock meets the frame.

Mrs Doran tests it twice. Elias picks up his mug.

MRS DORAN
Work?

ELIAS
For the moment.

She notices his jacket, but lets the answer stand.

He takes her trolley. They descend together. At the lobby she retrieves the handle.

ELIAS
You still have my screwdriver.

MRS DORAN
You gave it to me.

ELIAS
I lent it.

MRS DORAN
That was in 2019.

ELIAS
So you admit possession.

She heads out. Elias waits until she reaches the pavement. He checks his watch. Later still. He goes on toward the mailboxes.

= SC003 — The clamp (accepted direction, DEC-0014)

INT./EXT. ELIAS'S BUILDING/CAR PARK - MORNING

A FINAL NOTICE juts from Elias's overfull mailbox. He pushes it back and steps outside. A delivery van, a car with one bad brake light and a terrier pulling at the kerb pass through his field of view. Nothing demands action; he notices anyway. At the next street his hatchback wears a yellow sticker and a wheel clamp.

ELIAS
You have got to be joking.

The permit is visible. The notice names the wrong street. Elias photographs the permit, bay, notice, street sign and clamp before he opens his boot.

He checks the disc and battery of a compact angle grinder. Sparks jump as he cuts only the clamp's replaceable arm. He waits for the disc to stop, places both pieces neatly by the sign and photographs them. The sticker goes into the passenger footwell. The grinder returns to its case with the battery removed.

He starts the reluctant engine and tests the brake before pulling out. His notice to Mac remains in his jacket. A man watches from across the street, but Elias has already gone.

= SC004 — The call (accepted direction, DEC-0015)

INT. ELIAS'S CAR - CITY TRAFFIC - MORNING

The light turns red after only three cars pass. Elias angles across a speed hump; pain catches at his ribs. Coffee leaks over his fingers. He wipes them on his trousers.

On his phone: INSURANCE RENEWAL — TODAY. RENT — OVERDUE. He dismisses each. A missed call from the prescription clinic holds his attention. He turns the phone face down. Mac's letter rests against his ribs inside his jacket.

Sirens. A police car crosses ahead, then an ambulance, then another police car. Elias brakes hard; the belt catches his ribs. The ambulance travels between them. No visible collision equipment on the trailing car. He watches until the buildings hide them.

The X-Files ringtone. MR PRESIDENT on the screen. Elias answers.

ELIAS
Sir, traffic's a nightmare. I'm—

MAC (V.O.)
Don't come to the office. I need you at the airport. Immediately.

ELIAS
What happened to the assigned driver?

MAC (V.O.)
Unavailable. You're closer to the airport than anyone else I trust.

ELIAS
What am I collecting?

MAC (V.O.)
My niece. Zoe Harper. Thirteen. Travelling alone.

ELIAS
Where are her parents?

MAC (V.O.)
With family. Take her to her grandmother.

Elias touches the envelope in his jacket.

ELIAS
That doesn't answer the question. Why is she alone?

MAC (V.O.)
The adults responsible made that arrangement. Find her. Keep her safe. Drive.

A horn sounds behind Elias. The light is green. He stays still.

ELIAS
What flight? Does she know who's meeting her?

MAC (V.O.)
I'll send it. She has your photograph.

The call ends. Elias looks at the unopened letter, then at the airport turn on his navigation. Thirteen. Alone. He puts the car into gear. A flight number and an address three hours from the airport arrive. No photo of Zoe or family contact yet. Elias indicates left.

# SEQ-02 — THE GIRL WHO SETS TERMS

= SC005 — Processing (accepted direction, DEC-0016)

EXT. AIRPORT SHORT STAY CAR PARK - DAY

Elias parks beneath the flight path. The ticket machine prints a costly short-stay ticket. His phone shows LANDED and Mac's brief messages: ZOE HARPER. THIRTEEN. FAMILY COLLECTION. An address three hours away. No photograph, grandmother's number or escort details. He calls Mac; voicemail. Pain catches under his ribs as he reaches toward the glove compartment. He stops, pockets the ticket and heads for Arrivals with his notice still in his jacket.

INT. AIRPORT BORDER CONTROL/CORRIDOR - DAY

ZOE, thirteen, grips a small backpack. A WOMAN in a plain navy coat and unmarked lanyard walks beside her with a folder. At the desk, the woman answers before Zoe can.

OFFICER
Purpose of visit? How long?

ZOE
Family visit. Two weeks.

The officer stamps her passport. The woman leads her past the families waiting behind the glass.

WOMAN
Your uncle's driver is waiting. You were shown his photograph.

ZOE
That doesn't make him anything. You were given instructions too.

At the final doors the woman points into the public hall. Zoe waits for her to follow. The woman stays back and touches Zoe between the shoulders. Zoe steps away.

ZOE
Don't touch me.

People glance over, then return to their journeys. Zoe walks through alone.

INT. AIRPORT ARRIVALS - DAY

Elias stands where he can see the doors and exits. His phone finally receives Zoe's photograph and Mac's message: SHE HAS YOUR PHOTO. THIS MAN LOOKS HARMLESS. THAT IS WHY I CHOSE HIM. Elias looks up. Zoe is already still in the moving crowd, bag against her chest. Her eyes check exits, uniforms, hands, then him.

The woman in navy sees them both and leaves by a side corridor. No introduction, identification check or public handover. Elias watches her go. Zoe notices him noticing. He steps clear of the crowd and leaves space between them.

ELIAS
Zoe? I'm Elias. Your uncle sent me.

ZOE
Show me the message.

Elias takes out his phone and turns the screen toward her without moving closer.

= SC006 — Terms at arrivals (accepted direction, DEC-0017)

INT. AIRPORT ARRIVALS - DAY

Zoe stays beyond arm's reach while Elias holds up his phone. She reads Mac's name, the collection message and the destination address, then compares his face with the photograph on her own phone.

ZOE
Scroll up. More. Show me the photograph he sent you.

Elias does. Zoe studies the picture of herself and nods once. A completed check, not consent.

ZOE
Don't talk unless I speak first. Don't ask questions. Don't tell Mac anything I say. Don't touch me. Don't walk behind me. Walk in front, with your hands where I can see them.

ELIAS
I won't touch you or walk behind you. I won't ask about your journey here. And I don't report conversations to Mac as a hobby.

ZOE
But?

ELIAS
If something becomes a safety problem, it becomes mine.

ZOE
I didn't agree to that.

ELIAS
You don't have to.

Neither pretends the terms are settled. Zoe tightens her grip on her bag.

ZOE
Walk.

INT. AIRPORT PEDESTRIAN BRIDGE/LIFT - DAY

Elias walks ahead, leaving several paces. In the closed currency exchange glass, he sees Zoe checking the reflections and keeping clear of people who cross behind her. He does not turn to inspect her.

An AIRPORT EMPLOYEE directs them to the lift. He asks if they are together. Elias looks to Zoe; she answers for herself.

ZOE
Yes.

At the lift, Zoe waits until Elias has entered, then chooses a corner where she can see the doors and the metal wall's reflection. A LITTLE GIRL holds a toy rabbit with one ear repaired in blue thread.

LITTLE GIRL
Are you going on holiday?

ZOE
I just came back.

The girl's mother apologises. The girl lifts the rabbit.

LITTLE GIRL
He was scared of the plane. But I held him.

Zoe studies the repaired ear. The girl and her mother leave at their floor. Zoe watches the rabbit go. Elias faces the doors and says nothing. At the car park level he steps out first; Zoe follows once she has checked the landing.

= SC007 — The car test

EXT./INT. AIRPORT CAR PARK/ELIAS'S CAR - DAY

Zoe keeps behind Elias as he unlocks the hatchback. She reads the number plate, checks under neighbouring cars, then peers beneath the rear seat and under the car. He opens the boot when she asks: blanket, oil, tool case, emergency water. She keeps him in view throughout.

ZOE
Child lock.

ELIAS
Off. Test it.

She sits with her backpack on, closes the door, opens it from inside, and tests it again. Elias steps away. He shows her the parking ticket and walks toward the payment machine. As he presses the central lock, her face changes. He unlocks it again.

ELIAS
The doors stay unlocked. Two minutes. Don't touch the controls.

At the machine the ticket fails. Zoe watches him, then the ramp. She slips into the front seat. The old steering-column housing hangs loose; she draws out an already damaged connection and works it. Dashboard lights. The starter clicks, then the engine catches. She reaches for the pedals, backpack still strapped to her.

The car rolls backwards toward the ramp. Elias runs. Zoe corrects too sharply, clipping a concrete ridge. Elias pulls open the unlocked passenger door, steps onto the sill, and strikes his injured shoulder on the roof. Zoe shields her face with an elbow. He reaches past her, without touching her, and pulls the handbrake. The car stops. The engine stalls.

ELIAS
The car is stopped. Foot off the clutch.

Zoe withdraws her leg. Elias backs away from the open door. A PARKING ATTENDANT approaches. Elias starts to call it a family disagreement, catches himself, then states that Zoe tried to move the car and nobody is hurt. Asked directly whether she is all right, Zoe looks at the open route to the lift, then answers for herself.

ZOE
I'm all right.

Outside, her legs shake. Elias leaves both car doors open.

ELIAS
You weren't stealing it. You were getting away from me.

She says nothing. He points to the terminal.

ELIAS
We can go back inside, tell airport police you don't want to leave with me. Or I can drive you to the address Mac sent. I won't force you into the car. I won't leave you alone up here either.

ZOE
Both choices go through adults.

ELIAS
Yes. That doesn't make them safe.

Zoe glances at the lift. If she goes back, she says, they will call the number in the escort's folder. She will not say whose number. Elias does not press her.

ELIAS
If you come, sit in back. Door unlocked. Ask to stop. Don't touch the controls again. You can watch the destination on the navigation.

ZOE
You can change it.

ELIAS
Yes.

ZOE
Keep your phone where I can see it. If you call Mac, stop first so I can get out.

Elias considers the condition. Families pass behind the pedestrian bridge glass. Zoe looks from the terminal to the open hatchback.

ZOE
Back seat.

ELIAS
Not agreement. Understood.

He reconnects the old taped ignition block while she watches his hands. The engine catches. The attendant prints an exit receipt and checks on Zoe again. Elias does not claim she is fine.

ELIAS
She chose the car over the terminal.

ZOE
I chose.

She gets in behind him, keeps her backpack, and checks the inside handle once more.

= SC008 — The public stop

INT./EXT. ELIAS'S CAR/MOTORWAY REFUGE - DAY

Elias mounts his phone where Zoe can see the destination. The route estimate climbs past six hours. From the rear seat she tests the handle and belt, then checks every airport turn against the blue line. Elias drives without hiding the screen.

On the motorway a dark saloon stays behind them through two junctions, then disappears behind a lorry. Zoe notices it. Elias has seen too little to identify its occupants. A withheld number rings; he lets it go. Zoe watches him keep the phone in its mount.

Heat fills the old car. Zoe swallows, pales and finally leans forward.

ZOE
I feel sick.

Elias indicates for the next emergency refuge. Zoe reaches for the belt before they stop.

ELIAS
Keep it on until we're stopped. Then you can open the door.

The hatchback stops. Zoe gets out with her backpack and retches by the barrier. Elias puts on the hazards, places a sealed bottle of water where she can see it, and stays by the car. She checks the cap before drinking.

ELIAS
Next services. Food, cameras, staff, people.

ZOE
People don't help.

ELIAS
Sometimes they complicate things.

Back on the road, the withheld number calls again. Elias rejects it. Zoe hears him refuse the call while moving. A tiny laugh escapes her at his dry reply about making Mac wait. He does not turn to catch it.

EXT./INT. SERVICE STATION/RESTAURANT - AFTERNOON

The dark saloon enters half a minute after them and disappears behind the coaches. Elias circles the car park and stops beneath cameras, in sight of the restaurant. He shows Zoe the public exits and kitchen access on the posted fire diagram. She insists on seeing the phone if he makes a call. He agrees to tell her first.

Inside THE YE OLD BARN, a WAITRESS offers a booth or window table. Zoe wants sight of outside; Elias points out the booth's mirrored view of the entrance and toilet corridor. Zoe chooses the booth and her side of it. Her backpack stays beside her, a strap around her wrist.

Zoe is vegetarian. The waitress recommends a baked potato with beans, no butter, and sealed water. Elias orders pie and tea. When a butter packet arrives on Zoe's plate, the waitress replaces the whole plate on a clean tray.

ZOE
Thank you.

She eats while watching the mirror. For a moment Elias's unpressed jokes bring a smile. The room keeps moving around them: families, drivers, staff. Zoe speaks again, quieter.

ZOE
I thought the route was compromised before the airport. There was a decoy.

ELIAS
Who knew the real one?

Her face changes. Elias lets the question go. She looks at the woman serving them, then back at him.

ZOE
Do you believe me?

ELIAS
I believe you think it was exposed. I believe someone followed us from the airport. And I believe you had a reason to run before you knew whether I was dangerous.

ZOE
Trust needs proof?

ELIAS
Yes. It's the only kind worth having.

Zoe tests that answer in silence. A figure crosses the far edge of the car park. Elias sees it in the window reflection, but cannot identify anyone yet.

= SC009 — Cain's men

INT. THE YE OLD BARN RESTAURANT - AFTERNOON

Elias watches the mirrored entrance. Zoe has just spoken of a compromised route. At the edge of the car park a tall shape crosses too far away to identify.

ELIAS
I know what Cain does to people who think they can leave.

ZOE
Cain.

Elias's fork stops. His shoulders settle; his breathing slows. Zoe sees the ordinary tired driver become precise.

ZOE
How do you know him? Does Mac?

ELIAS
I've crossed paths with some of his people. I don't know what Mac knows.

ZOE
You drive for him. That's where you want responsibility to stop.

His phone lights: PRIVATE NUMBER. He rejects it twice. It rings a third time. He answers.

CALLER (V.O.)
Send the girl outside and you can leave alive.

The line dies. Elias sets the phone down. In the window reflection two men stand beneath the entrance awning. One watches the car park; the other uses the black glass to watch inside. The saloon sits near the coaches, at least one person still inside. Others may be out of view.

ELIAS
Two at the door. One in the car. I can't count anyone hidden.

Zoe begins to turn. Elias indicates the mirror. She sees them without facing them directly. He slides cash under his plate.

ELIAS
Go to the ladies' toilets for three minutes. At the corridor, check the fire-door window. If someone follows, return to the dining room and tell the waitress. Don't let them put you inside with them.

ZOE
You're separating us. What if you don't return?

ELIAS
Come back to this table. The cash covers the bill. Don't go outside with anyone claiming I sent them. If I don't return, ask the waitress to call airport police. Tell them about the escort and the flight, not the number in your travel folder.

Zoe grips her backpack. She looks at the mirrored men and the open restaurant. Elias meets her eyes without promising he will be back.

ELIAS
Repeat it.

ZOE
Check the window. Lock the main door. Three minutes. Back to the table. Pay, no change. Waitress and airport police if you don't return. Trust no messenger.

ELIAS
Good.

ZOE
Was that praise?

ELIAS
Confirmation.

Zoe stands, steadies herself on the table without his help, and walks to the corridor at an ordinary pace. She checks the window and glances back. Elias's tea still steams. His seat is already empty.

= SC010 — Three minutes

INT. SERVICE-STATION TOILETS/RESTAURANT - AFTERNOON

Zoe bolts the main toilet door. The sound catches her. She unbolts it, bolts it again, then checks the cubicles and narrow frosted window. Her watch counts down from three minutes. Kitchen workers pass outside with rubbish bags; ordinary complaints travel through the glass.

She looks at an unanswered message to JACKIE. Her hand hovers over the name. She locks her phone without calling. Her expression hardens, then falters. When the final second passes, Zoe unlocks the door herself.

She checks the corridor window. Back at the booth, Elias's half-eaten pie and cash remain; his chair is empty. Zoe sits in her original place, keeps her bag close, and watches the awning through the mirror. The WAITRESS brings the bill and asks if the man with her is returning.

ZOE
He isn't my dad. He said to pay. Keep the change.

The waitress asks if all is well. Zoe says yes while watching a man touch an earpiece. Another shifts to cover the car. A third near the portable toilets turns toward the service yard. Their positions tighten, then hesitate. Zoe has no view of Elias.

A chair moves opposite her. Elias is back, dusty, a thin line of blood on one knuckle. Zoe nearly reacts.

ELIAS
Don't. Walk on my left. Normally.

ZOE
You're bleeding.

ELIAS
Not mine.

INT./EXT. RESTAURANT/SERVICE-STATION CAR PARK - CONTINUOUS

Elias walks toward the doors at an ordinary pace. Zoe takes his left. A waiter with plates, a child with a drink and two lorry drivers interrupt their path. Neither rushes. Outside, two men keep their hands visible but watch Zoe like a target. A football rolls across her path; she steps over it.

From between the coaches comes a TALL MAN in a black jacket. Zoe recognises the authority he had at Cain's house from how others watch him. He sees Elias and loses colour. His hand moves away from his pocket.

TALL MAN
Elias.

Elias keeps walking. Zoe almost stops; he matches her shortened stride, then leads her around a parked van. The tall man calls again but does not pursue. A man near their car moves to narrow the approach.

Zoe's backpack catches on the rear door frame. She frees it, gets in and pulls the door shut. Elias sits behind the wheel. The central locks drop; Zoe tests the inside handle. It still works. The engine coughs twice before catching. An estate car blocks their reverse path for a pedestrian; Elias waits rather than forcing through. A pursuer touches the roof, then recoils after looking into Elias's face.

The lane clears. Elias reverses and drives with control. The men run toward a dark saloon and a black four-by-four. The saloon's tyres collapse under it; the four-by-four drops at one corner and stops at a divider. Zoe sees only the effect. The tall man remains behind with a phone to his ear. The hatchback reaches the slip road.

ZOE
He knew you. What did you do to those cars?

ELIAS
Delayed them. I'll explain what I can when we're clear.

His injured hand tightens on the wheel. Zoe looks back once, then at the unlocked handle.

= SC011 — Seven days

INT. ELIAS'S CAR - MOTORWAY/INDUSTRIAL ROADS - DAY

The hatchback heads east though Mac's address lies west. Zoe checks the visible navigation.

ZOE
Wrong way.

ELIAS
They expect west.

He tests a junction, changes roads and eventually rejoins westbound traffic elsewhere. A white van leaves after several miles; a motorcycle overtakes. Nothing repeats long enough to confirm a tail. Zoe audits each turn.

She sees the cut on Elias's hand. He admits a man grabbed him behind the restaurant and that he damaged tyres on two pursuit vehicles with something from his tool kit. He refuses to describe the method.

ELIAS
It bought minutes, not safety. It isn't a lesson.

PRIVATE NUMBER rings three times. Elias rejects it while driving. A message appears in the dash mount: THE REAPER SHOULD HAVE STAYED DEAD. His grip tightens, then steadies. Zoe has seen it.

ZOE
Are you dangerous?

ELIAS
Yes.

ZOE
To me?

ELIAS
No.

ZOE
How do I know?

ELIAS
You don't.

She studies his face in the mirror. He says he intervened because Cain does not get to take another child if he can stop it. Zoe objects to “child”; Elias agrees that it does not make her helpless or stupid. He lists what she noticed about the flight and the collection before he did.

ELIAS
Mac told me you were his niece.

ZOE
That doesn't mean he believes it.

His eyes move to her backpack. Zoe pulls it closer, then admits she took something from Cain. She will not show him. He stops asking. The phone stays dark in its mount.

ELIAS
I won't call Mac without telling you first. If the danger is immediate, I act. Otherwise, I tell you.

ZOE
That's a condition, not forever.

ELIAS
Agreed.

Zoe looks at the route. It still points toward the supposed grandmother; Elias has not decided to follow it.

ZOE
Where are we going?

ELIAS
For now, we keep moving. I need the beginning before I choose.

She holds the bag. Elias waits without prompting. Finally she sets her terms: she will keep its contents private, may not tell everything, and wants to finish each day before his questions.

ELIAS
Each day?

ZOE
There were seven. Day one was Tuesday.

The road noise carries into the first memory.

# SEQ-03 — WHAT HAPPENED TUESDAY TO FRIDAY

= SC012 — The ordinary errand

INT. ELIAS'S CAR - MOTORWAY - PRESENT DAY

Zoe keeps her backpack on her lap. The road passes beneath them.

ZOE
It was an ordinary Tuesday. I hadn't arranged to meet anyone. I was walking home.

Elias listens. The sound of traffic carries into her memory.

INT./EXT. HELEN AND BRIAN'S HOUSE/SCHOOL - TUESDAY - ZOE'S ACCOUNT

HELEN calls up the stairs. ZOE, thirteen, appears with one shoe on. BRIAN has already left. Over burned toast Helen teases her about the missing shoe. At school JACKIE returns Zoe's science book, then joins her on the wall behind the sports hall. Their shared joke about a teacher lasts until the final bell. Jackie crosses the road first.

JACKIE
Message me when you get home.

ZOE
You message me.

Jackie smiles. This is a familiar argument with no stakes yet.

INT./EXT. HELEN'S KITCHEN/CORNER SHOP/STREET - LATE AFTERNOON - ZOE'S ACCOUNT

Helen discovers there is no milk and gives Zoe money. Zoe negotiates an extra pound for chocolate. Four familiar streets separate the shop and home. She pays for a milk bottle and chocolate, then walks past the boarded florist, post box and stone dog.

A white unmarked van slows beside her. The side door opens. Two adults pull Zoe inside. The milk strikes the pavement and spreads along the kerb. The door slams before she can see home.

INT. VAN/GARAGE/HOUSE - TUESDAY - ZOE'S ACCOUNT

Held inside the moving van, Zoe stops kicking to breathe. She counts a left turn, a long road, a roundabout, two close speed bumps and a right, restarting whenever fear breaks her count. The van enters a garage; a shutter rattles closed before she is led out.

Through a front-door window: a trimmed lawn and a child's bicycle. Inside: drawn curtains, a locked cabinet of numbered folders. Zoe glimpses a safe through a partly open study door before a man shuts it.

A WOMAN sets down a glass of water Zoe did not request.

WOMAN
You'll be processed tomorrow.

ZOE
For what?

No answer. Upstairs Zoe checks an empty wardrobe, a high window that barely opens, and a lock on the outside of her door. Alone on the bed, she hears footsteps and returns her gaze to the milk image in her memory.

INT. ELIAS'S CAR - MOTORWAY - PRESENT DAY

ELIAS
Nobody had arranged to meet you? What could you see on the journey?

ZOE
I was buying milk. I counted turns. Not enough to find the place from the route. It was still light when we arrived.

ELIAS
Did you know Cain's name then?

ZOE
Not yet.

He lets the silence stand, then checks the mirror.

ELIAS
Keep going when you're ready. I'm listening.

Zoe decides to continue.

= SC013 — The rules

INT. CONTROLLED HOUSE - WEDNESDAY - ZOE'S ACCOUNT

Zoe wakes in a smaller room. White-painted bars cross the window. Folded grey clothes have replaced her own. A WOMAN recites rules without knocking: speak only when spoken to, no questions, no looking the buyers in the eye. Zoe asks what a buyer is. The woman leaves.

Downstairs six children occupy a room with too many chairs. A younger girl flinches when a man passes. He crouches and speaks too quietly to hear; she goes still. Two older children whisper CAIN's name. When an adult appears, the room falls silent.

Later a man enters. No one introduces him. Adults step aside and straighten. Cain passes into the study without looking at the children. The woman gives Zoe a jug to fill. Through the study door Zoe hears fragments: a confirmed buyer, pending documentation, an older file under its original number. She cannot see who the buyer is.

At the kitchen sink a phone call draws the woman away from a numbered folder. On top lies a photograph of Zoe at six or seven, laughing. Water spills over Zoe's hand as she recognises herself. She looks once at the number and returns to the children's room without touching the file.

INT. ELIAS'S CAR - MOTORWAY - PRESENT DAY

ELIAS
You knew it had your picture. Did you know what the number meant?

ZOE
No. I didn't know who kept it or why. I knew Cain was the one they feared.

Elias holds the rest of his question. Zoe continues.

INT. CONTROLLED HOUSE - WEDNESDAY/THURSDAY - ZOE'S ACCOUNT

A bedside light stays on. Each time Zoe drifts, a door opens: water, a window check, a room change, another question about school or home phrased slightly differently. A television murmurs below, always too low to follow. She counts stairs, a clicking floorboard and footsteps to keep something fixed as the days blur.

On Thursday children are separated. Clothes arrive in transparent bags; adults inspect hair, nails and teeth. The tall man from Cain's house stands in the doorway, cigarette smell preceding him. The others hold still until he leaves.

Two women discuss tomorrow and whether Zoe will be “settled.” Later an OLDER GIRL sits beside Zoe's bed and whispers without turning.

OLDER GIRL
Tomorrow's the showing. Don't eat or drink anything they give you beforehand.

Zoe looks at her. A door opens; the girl moves away. At the next offered cup Zoe takes it and sets it down untouched when no one is looking.

INT. ELIAS'S CAR - MOTORWAY - PRESENT DAY

ELIAS
We can stop the account.

ZOE
I want to go on. I've been carrying it alone.

He lets her decide when to speak again. The road holds their silence.

= SC014 — The showing and escape

INT. CONTROLLED HOUSE - FRIDAY - ZOE'S ACCOUNT

An older WOMAN brings a paper cup before the showing. Zoe remembers the warning, raises it, holds the bitter liquid behind her teeth while the woman watches, then spits it into a sink and rinses. She cannot know whether she swallowed some. When adults return with her cleaned school uniform, Zoe moves slowly and lets her focus drift. She notices their keys and corridor checks while they treat her as sedated.

INT. SHOWING ROOM - FRIDAY - ZOE'S ACCOUNT

Bright panels, a square of pale tape and dark glass. Zoe is placed on the square. Voices from behind the glass ask about original and placement documentation, medication and travel. A light crosses her teeth. She fixes her eyes on a chip at the bottom of the glass while the room tips slightly.

VOICE (O.S.)
This number doesn't match the transfer sheet.

Another voice insists the original record holds the first identifier. A dispute over access and payment rises beyond the glass. A door opens; the woman leaves. The speaker dies. Zoe remains on the taped square through several breaths, then chooses to step out. She catches the frame when her footing fails.

INT. CONTROLLED HOUSE - ARCHIVE/SERVICE PASSAGE - FRIDAY - ZOE'S ACCOUNT

The archive door and safe stand open. Numbered folders, a leather ledger, hard drives and a laptop crowd the room. On the desk: the childhood photograph and the number Zoe saw on Wednesday. A clear property bag holds her school things and backpack. She takes her folder and belongings. A small black drive is connected to the laptop beside her record. She cannot read the screen, but pulls the drive free and packs it too. The laptop chimes. She hides as an injured man's escort passes the doorway.

In a service passage two men move an injured person through a door held open by a crate. Their attention is elsewhere. Zoe slips outside; her backpack catches briefly and she frees it. She runs toward traffic, hides behind bins until she can count ten breaths, and finds a bus stop with a town-centre destination she recognises.

A DRIVER sees her school uniform and waves her aboard despite her having no fare. At the interchange she changes to a familiar route, keeps her bag close and passes the school stop. She stays aboard for home. At her own gate she hesitates, then knocks.

INT. ELIAS'S CAR - MOTORWAY - PRESENT DAY

ELIAS
You knew the folder was yours?

ZOE
I knew the photograph and number. I didn't understand the rest until the night before the flight. I still haven't properly opened the drive.

ELIAS
Why take it?

ZOE
They cared enough to fight over the archive. I thought it might tell me who I was.

He looks at her in the mirror. She still holds the backpack.

ELIAS
You were frightened. You still chose to take your file and get out. Both can be true.

Her grip loosens a little.

ZOE
There's more.

ELIAS
Take your time.

# SEQ-04 — HOME, DECOY AND WRONG DESTINATION

= SC015 — Home is changed

INT. HELEN AND BRIAN'S HOUSE - FRIDAY EVENING - ZOE'S ACCOUNT

Helen opens before Zoe can knock twice. She pulls Zoe inside and holds her until Zoe's knees nearly give. BRIAN looks past them to the street.

BRIAN
Did anyone see you? Follow you?

He locks and chains the door, checks it again, then turns off the porch light. The photographs, old shoes and Zoe's ceramic hedgehog are where she left them. The familiar room feels watched.

Helen asks if Zoe is hurt and offers to call police and a doctor. Brian asks what they will say about Zoe's papers and calls MAC first. Zoe refuses to surrender her backpack. Helen stops reaching for it. Later she cleans a scrape on Zoe's wrist and makes toast; Zoe can eat half a slice. Brian returns with Mac's order: no school, messages, Jackie, police or outside contact before he arrives.

Zoe locks her bedroom door, hides the folder and drive in her bag beneath the bed, and leaves them unread. At night voices rise through the heating vent.

BRIAN (O.S.)
The money won't matter if this comes out. What will we say about no proper adoption? Who arranged it?

HELEN (O.S.)
We raised her.

Zoe studies the childhood photographs, now unable to read them as reassurance.

INT. HELEN AND BRIAN'S LIVING ROOM/KITCHEN - SATURDAY - ZOE'S ACCOUNT

Mac arrives before nine. Helen and Brian quieten as he asks Zoe about the escape, the buses and anyone she contacted. She keeps the folder to herself. He orders no police, social services or hospital except through him. Helen protests that Zoe needs care. He calls the restriction protection.

MAC
She'll go to my mother. Her grandmother.

ZOE
Helen told me I didn't have one. Why do they listen to you?

Mac silences Brian with a raised finger. He will not answer beyond saying he knows what happens next. Before leaving he forbids Zoe to contact Jackie.

Afterward Helen reaches toward Zoe. Zoe steps back, seeing that her hurt is real.

ZOE
Why didn't you call the police when I disappeared? How did you search for me?

Helen cannot describe it. Brian says they did what they could without making matters worse. Zoe holds her backpack between them.

INT. ELIAS'S CAR - MOTORWAY - PRESENT DAY

ZOE
I think they loved me. That's why I can't throw it all away. I don't know which parts were real.

ELIAS
You may never get one clean answer. That doesn't mean you imagined the good parts. It doesn't erase what they failed to do.

Zoe remembers Jackie telling an old story: a watcher sent between rival crews, the only one to walk away when both attacked. She says the date. Elias eases the car into another lane and checks the mirror twice.

ELIAS
Interesting story.

ZOE
You've heard it before.

He does not answer. Zoe sees enough to register a connection, not its explanation.

= SC016 — Jackie and the decoy

INT. HELEN AND BRIAN'S HOUSE - SUNDAY - ZOE'S ACCOUNT

At the chained front door, JACKIE demands to hear Zoe say she does not want to see her. Zoe comes downstairs. Jackie's sharpness disappears.

JACKIE
Purple penguin.

ZOE
Green giraffe.

Helen removes the chain. Jackie holds Zoe, then brings food and two phones upstairs. She divides portions before thinking, with one wrapped for later. She speaks of the three younger siblings she used to feed after their mother died, now in different placements. She does not speak of them in the past tense.

Zoe tells her about the house and shows the edge of her folder and black drive without handing them over. Jackie asks about doors, windows and names. Neither can verify what the drive contains.

On the back of a science worksheet they write SEE, FIND and BELIEVE. Using mobile data and an old shared account, they leave a coach timetable, ferry route and incomplete booking trail that a watcher might discover. The false route leads away from the airport. There is no evidence yet that anyone saw it.

Helen finds them and orders Jackie out. Jackie says she will create a police record of Zoe's disappearance if forced away. Brian yields: Jackie may stay until after ten. Brian then brings Mac's real boarding pass and Elias's photograph. On its back: HE IS HARMLESS. DO EXACTLY WHAT HE SAYS. The pickup is before dawn.

The girls revise the decoy. Jackie will wear Zoe's coat and spare bag, board the coach, leave before the station and call from a public supermarket. If followed, she will seek police among people. She memorises the real itinerary; they write no paper copy of it.

ZOE
If you don't call, the route's exposed. I don't get in the car.

JACKIE
Then make noise. Go to a neighbour. Don't let them move you quietly.

Jackie repairs Zoe's pencil-case zip while they wait. At 22:06 she leaves by the back gate, hood up. Zoe and Jackie exchange two fingers across the distance. Zoe watches the spare phone at 22:30, 23:00, 23:30. No call.

INT. ELIAS'S CAR - MOTORWAY - PRESENT DAY

ZOE
I gave her the real route. Trust got her taken.

ELIAS
You made the plan together. She came because you vanished and stayed because she chose to. Whoever watched made their own choice afterward.

Zoe keeps looking at the silent phone in her memory. She does not know where Jackie is or why the call failed.

= SC017 — The flight

INT. HELEN AND BRIAN'S HOUSE - MONDAY PRE-DAWN - ZOE'S ACCOUNT

At midnight the spare phone still has no call from Jackie. Zoe tells Helen and Brian the agreed rule: no call means the real route is exposed. Helen says Mac's trip was already arranged. Brian takes the phone and switches it off. They refuse to call police. Zoe looks from the chained front door to Brian at the back door, weighing the noise Jackie told her to make.

In her room Zoe opens the folder for the first time. The childhood photograph and repeated number. ORIGINAL and ASSIGNED IDENTITY fields. Medical notes. A payment record. Mac's legal name as authorising client. Helen and Brian named as receiving placement. She finds no adoption order in this folder. She looks up at real photographs of the life she has lived with them. The folder changes her understanding of its arrangement, not the fact those years happened.

Helen brings clean clothes. Zoe says the trip is unsafe; Helen admits it may be and still prepares her for it. Zoe packs the folder and still-unopened black drive beneath ordinary things. One backpack, no suitcase. At 04:10 a car arrives. Helen embraces her and whispers an apology without naming what she means. Brian opens the door. Zoe goes out because travel may offer an opening she cannot find inside the house.

INT./EXT. CAR/DEPARTURES/AIRCRAFT - MONDAY - ZOE'S ACCOUNT

The unnamed DRIVER takes her to an airport. A navy-coated ESCORT with a folder receives the travel documents. At check-in and security, prefilled answers and a signed consent form pass through ordinary procedure. Zoe watches her bag travel through the scanner and takes it back immediately. No staff member is shown knowing what she carries or why she fears the destination.

The escort hands Zoe Elias's photograph and leaves before the gate. Asked the supposed grandmother's name, she refers Zoe back to Mac's arrangement instead of answering. Zoe boards with the backpack under the seat, one shoe through its strap. She studies Mac's handwriting on the photograph: HE IS HARMLESS. DO EXACTLY WHAT HE SAYS.

The aircraft lifts. Zoe sees no attempt to stop her. She does not know whether anyone has the real route, only that Jackie knew it and failed to call. She decides to run at the first opening after arrival.

INT. ELIAS'S CAR - MOTORWAY - PRESENT DAY

ZOE
Harmless meant controllable. Your photograph helped me identify you. It didn't make me trust you.

ELIAS
He sent me yours and said it would help you trust me.

They both understand how little those photographs established.

= SC018 — No grandmother

INT. ELIAS'S CAR - MOTORWAY - PRESENT DAY

Zoe tells Elias what she read: her original and assigned identity fields, payments, Mac's legal name beside AUTHORISING CLIENT, Helen and Brian as RECEIVING PLACEMENT. She found no adoption order in the folder. She believes Mac's uncle story was false and the supposed grandmother is no safe destination.

ZOE
He sent someone who would follow the address and not ask what was waiting there.

The exit for that address approaches. Elias keeps driving past it.

ELIAS
We're not going there.

He cancels the navigation. Zoe watches the blank map, then him.

ZOE
That doesn't mean you get to choose the next place for me.

ELIAS
No. We'll stop and decide what comes next.

EXT. PUBLIC REST AREA - EVENING

Elias parks facing one exit with another road visible. No vehicle follows them in. He leaves the doors unlocked and opens an old road atlas across the roof. A cottage is marked under a name he no longer uses. He says it was his; he cannot certify that Mac or Cain will never find it.

They consider police, hospital, waiting in public, a train or bus, and an uncertain ally. Zoe rejects easy assurances about any of them. Elias offers to check the cottage and leave if it is compromised. The folder and drive stay with her; he will not open either without her. He will not call Mac or move her in her sleep. Zoe chooses the slower of two routes in the atlas.

ELIAS
I treated you as a destination on a job.

ZOE
Don't do it again.

He accepts that. Zoe chooses the front passenger seat, the backpack still on her lap.

INT./EXT. ELIAS'S CAR/ROADSIDE FUEL STOP - NIGHT

Mac's X-Files ringtone interrupts. A message follows: YOU DO NOT UNDERSTAND WHAT YOU ARE CARRYING. Zoe reads it; Elias rejects the call. At the boot, he breaks the SIM and seals the handset in a signal-blocking pouch. Neither can know whether the car itself can still be found.

Zoe navigates from the outdated atlas, correcting for a new roundabout. Elias offers his jacket when she is cold; she takes it later. He asks what name she wants used despite the folder's other identity field.

ZOE
Zoe.

ELIAS
Then Zoe.

At a public petrol station she chooses to come inside. She swaps his cheese for hummus, and he pays cash. She eats beside the car while he fuels it. They leave on the route she selected, with the cottage still a provisional destination.

= SC019 — Jackie, unseen by Zoe

INT. WATCHED HOUSE - NIGHT

JACKIE sits at a kitchen table, visibly exhausted since the night she left Zoe's house. A camera covers the front entrance. Another looks toward the rear gate. The doors are ordinary doors.

A WATCHER sets a tablet on the table. On it, three children appear in brief, recent-looking moving images. Jackie leans forward despite herself. The watcher takes the tablet back before she can touch it.

JACKIE
They've changed.

WATCHER
You know what to do when we ask.

He leaves with the screen. Jackie tests the kitchen window with her eyes, then looks toward the rear gate camera. She has room to walk out, and a reason to believe doing so would expose the children.

A phone sits on the counter within reach but watched. Jackie does not take it. She puts a glass of water beside it, exactly where it was before, and waits until the watcher has passed. She is alive and trying to preserve a way to communicate. Zoe does not see her.

# SEQ-05 — THE TRACKER AND THREE HUNTERS

= SC020 — The contract

INT. MAC'S OFFICE - EVENING

On Mac's tablet a dot has passed the grandmother exit and moved through minor roads. Elias's phone is dead, yet the dot continues. A concealed transmitter was fitted beneath the hatchback before the airport collection. Mac opens Elias's thin personnel file; its gaps do not surprise him.

Cain calls. His lieutenant saw Elias at the service station, recognised him and was afraid. Neither man says the suspected name on the line. Cain claims Zoe; Mac disputes him. When the call ends, Mac takes a second phone from a locked drawer.

VOSS asks about the target profile and why three contractors are needed. REYES hears the fee and stops laughing at the idea of one tired driver. VALE asks what Mac has kept out of the brief. At eight all three enter Mac's office and receive folders describing recovery of a protected minor from an unauthorised custodian.

Vale reads the signatures and delegated authority. A company names another company as placement provider. She asks who can decide for the child. Mac repeats the paperwork rather than giving an independent answer. Voss marks the thin profile. Reyes studies Elias's photograph.

VALE
What is she carrying?

MAC
Personal effects.

The contract also requires property intact. Mac admits it may contain confidential material. Voss sets check-ins and forbids a solo breach; Reyes dislikes the rule. Vale distributes operational phones. Their different reactions to risk are visible before they sign.

Mac supplies live vehicle tracking and a broad last location, not a confirmed building. He withholds Cain's lieutenant's fear and the unspoken name. Vale asks why Cain's people may attempt another recovery; Mac calls their role irrelevant.

VOSS
When the feed fails, we search around the last position. We don't treat the dot as a door.

The three depart separately. Mac watches the signal weaken into the uplands. Beneath Elias's car, a green indicator pulses once in the dark.

= SC021 — First lesson

EXT. OLD COTTAGE - TUESDAY - PRE-DAWN

The hatchback stops at a chained gate. Zoe wakes beneath Elias's jacket. He retrieves a key from his wallet lining.

ZOE
This is it?

ELIAS
This is the gate.

ZOE
That wasn't the question.

Beyond the gate: an orchard, a leaning barn, shuttered windows. The dashboard reads 1:43. Elias parks and takes the keys.

ELIAS
Stay here.

ZOE
You said I could decide.

ELIAS
You can stay in the car or stand behind me. You cannot walk into a building I haven't checked.

ZOE
That sounds like deciding for me.

ELIAS
It is.

She follows him to the door, backpack against her ribs. He checks inside with a torch. She waits outside.

INT. OLD COTTAGE - CONTINUOUS

Dust across unused doors. Cold stone. Mismatched chairs. A sheet over the sofa.

Elias lifts an old fuse switch. A yellow bulb comes on. Brown water coughs from the tap, then clears.

ZOE
When did you last come here?

ELIAS
Years.

ZOE
Why keep it?

ELIAS
I didn't know where else to put it.

ZOE
The cottage?

ELIAS
Myself.

He reaches for the kettle. Zoe lets the question rest.

ELIAS
Your bedroom locks from inside. The key is in the door. I'm down here.

ZOE
Do you have another key?

ELIAS
No.

ZOE
How do I know?

ELIAS
You don't. You can wedge the chair beneath the handle.

ZOE
I know.

ELIAS
I assumed you would.

At the bedroom window he shows her the lean-to, the barrel beneath its lower edge and the orchard beyond. Zoe checks the route herself.

ZOE
What if you can't wake me?

ELIAS
Out this way. Through the orchard. Not the track.

LATER: bread and hummus beneath the yellow bulb. Zoe eats. Elias leaves her to it.

Upstairs, she shuts her door. A chair scrapes across the floor. Elias turns out the downstairs light.

EXT. OLD COTTAGE - TUESDAY MORNING

Birds. Water running beyond the orchard.

Zoe checks her backpack before going downstairs. A note on the table: OUTSIDE. DO NOT LEAVE THE HOUSE. BACK DOOR UNLOCKED.

She opens the back door. Elias's boots protrude beneath the hatchback.

ELIAS
You were told not to leave the house.

ZOE
I'm touching the doorstep.

His tool stops. He reaches out for the torch. Zoe puts it in his hand.

Black adhesive on his fingertips. He frees a small black device from behind the rear wheel liner. A green pinprick pulses.

ZOE
That's the tracker.

ELIAS
Fresh tool marks. The service yard.

ZOE
The heat-shield repair.

He nods. She looks back at the cottage.

ZOE
So he knows where we are.

ELIAS
He knows where this last reported. The valley has almost no signal. It may have stopped above us. It may have stored the route.

ZOE
That is two versions of bad.

Elias opens the casing and disconnects the battery. The light dies. Device and battery go into separate coat pockets.

ZOE
He sent me with someone he didn't trust and tracked both of us.

ELIAS
He trusted the system around us.

ZOE
You said I chose the cottage. From options you gave me. We were in the car.

Elias stops working and faces her.

ELIAS
I should have found it sooner.

ZOE
Why didn't you?

ELIAS
I thought I understood the limits of how Mac controlled people.

ZOE
You knew he controlled people. And you worked for him.

ELIAS
Yes.

ZOE
That is worse than not knowing.

ELIAS
Yes.

She watches him. He holds her look.

INT. OLD COTTAGE - LATER

Elias draws the stream and a public footpath on the back of an expired tea packet. He adds the village to the east.

ELIAS
An hour to check the ridge. If I'm not back, wait another hour. Then take your bag and use the window.

He rigs a rope to lower the backpack first. Zoe checks where it reaches. He leaves; she locks the back door.

LATER: muddy boots at the threshold. Zoe has moved a chair so she can see both doors without standing in either.

ELIAS
Coverage dies below the ridge. My best guess is the last clean transmission was above the track.

ZOE
Best guess. You don't know.

ELIAS
No.

ZOE
Good. You keep saying when you don't know.

He touches the pocket with the device.

ELIAS
We keep it dead. Leaving it here would confirm the cottage. No phones. No car. No lights after dark.

ZOE
Teach me something useful.

INT. OLD COTTAGE - WEDNESDAY MORNING

The hatchback is inside the barn. At the front window, Elias opens a shutter a narrow distance.

ELIAS
Tell me what's outside.

ZOE
Track. Trees. Gate. Mud.

ELIAS
What's happening outside?

ZOE
That's a different question.

ELIAS
It's the useful one. Who belongs. What repeats. What changes.

Through the trees: only a short slice of public road. Vehicles will appear for seconds.

ZOE
You can't see enough.

ELIAS
That's the lesson. One hour. Order, direction, speed, anything that doesn't fit. No notebook.

ZOE
Why?

ELIAS
Looking down costs time.

She takes the chair.

ZOE
You're not watching?

ELIAS
Checking the back boundary.

ZOE
You'll have to believe me.

ELIAS
That's also the lesson.

THE WATCH: a red tractor climbs towards the fields with a trailer, returns without it. A blue car goes downhill. A postal van reverses awkwardly. Two walkers shorten their white dog's lead near the orchard.

A grey estate passes slowly. Cream interior. Something dark beneath its mirror. Its lone driver holds his face forward.

The clock advances three minutes.

The estate returns faster. The driver's eyes move to his mirror as he clears the entrance. His head stays still.

Zoe rises, looks at the clock, then stays at the window. The tractor returns with its trailer before Elias comes in.

ZOE
The tractor lost its trailer and came back for it. Blue car downhill. Post van. Walkers. Then a grey estate, twice.

ELIAS
Driver?

ZOE
One man. First time he didn't look anywhere.

Elias straightens.

ZOE
Second time he watched the entrance in his mirror. Cream seats. Something hanging beneath the mirror. Plate too dirty. Last two might be seven K. Or seven X.

ELIAS
Second pass. Did he slow?

ZOE
No. Do you know the car?

ELIAS
No.

ZOE
You believe me?

ELIAS
Yes.

He checks the empty road and closes the shutter.

ELIAS
We leave before first light tomorrow.

ZOE
Why not now?

ELIAS
We're exhausted. My best read is search, then a check of the access road. That doesn't mean they have this cottage. It doesn't give us long enough to stay.

ZOE
Mac's people? Cain's?

ELIAS
Possibly either.

He takes a holdall from the cupboard.

ZOE
You would have seen it anyway.

ELIAS
No.

She waits.

ELIAS
You noticed him trying not to look interested. Most people remember the slow car. You remembered the behaviour.

ZOE
And the mirror.

ELIAS
And the mirror.

He packs food, candles and the atlas. She takes the tea-packet map upstairs.

ZOE
Who knows the next place?

ELIAS
Fewer people than know this cottage.

ZOE
That isn't nobody.

ELIAS
We're finished using that word.

INT./EXT. OLD COTTAGE - THURSDAY - BEFORE DAWN

Zoe sleeps dressed, chair beneath the handle, bag beside the bed. Downstairs Elias listens as an unseen engine idles somewhere beyond the trees, then goes away. He stays still.

At 4:30 he wakes her. Her hand goes straight to the backpack.

They strip sheets and pack fresh rubbish. Zoe hands him the cottage key. He locks the door. They cross the orchard together.

The hatchback stays in the barn. Beyond the stream Elias uncovers an older car.

Zoe crouches beside a rear wheel.

ZOE
Does this one have a tracker?

ELIAS
We're about to find out.

He hands her the torch. They begin the sweep together.

= SC022 — Eastbound ghost

EXT. FREIGHT STOP - THURSDAY - BEFORE SUNRISE

The second car waits beyond the barrier. In the cup holder, Mac's transmitter pulses again. Zoe reads a route card on a detached refrigerated trailer.

ZOE
Whoever follows that follows the driver. What if they stop him?

ELIAS
There is risk.

ZOE
To somebody who knows nothing about us.

ELIAS
Yes.

She keeps the tracker in her hand. He waits.

ZOE
Use the detached trailer. Not a cab. Nothing that makes the driver look involved.

ELIAS
I can reduce the risk. I can't make it risk-free.

She hands it back. He fixes it to the trailer's rear structure. From the public road they watch a tractor unit collect it and head east. Only then does Elias turn west.

INT./EXT. FARMHOUSE - THURSDAY - DAY

A disused dairy yard. Poplars screen a road much closer than at the cottage. Elias checks water, both staircases and the drainage ditch before showing Zoe her bedroom. He gives her the second set of keys.

FRIDAY: rain drips onto the backpack. Elias finds Zoe upstairs.

ELIAS
Your bag's getting wet.

ZOE
You moved it?

ELIAS
No. It's yours.

She moves it herself. Later, her aligned zip pulls remain exactly as she left them.

SATURDAY: a road atlas and dried beans on the kitchen table. Zoe marks possible eastern depots.

ELIAS
Whoever is searching has choices.

She places one bean at the cottage, one east, one in the margin.

ZOE
One follows the signal. One stays where it disappeared. One follows who you used to be.

Elias studies the third bean.

ZOE
You keep paying for things you say you left behind.

ELIAS
The records search may be the dangerous one.

SUNDAY: Zoe struggles to mend the backpack strap.

ELIAS
May I?

She offers the loose end without releasing the bag. He takes only that end. His stitches are small and even.

ZOE
Who taught you?

ELIAS
Someone who got tired of repairing my work.

ZOE
Still in your life?

ELIAS
No.

She tests the strap. Later she leaves the backpack beneath the table. Elias does not touch it.

MONDAY EVENING: the beans return to the atlas.

ZOE
They've had four days. So they took it.

ELIAS
I think so.

ZOE
You said that like you knew.

ELIAS
I shouldn't have.

He reduces the claim: no repeated vehicle or approach here; the diversion probably worked. Zoe taps the records-search bean.

ZOE
And now?

ELIAS
More interested. Whoever Mac sent is good.

ZOE
So are we.

He leaves the answer standing.

= SC023 — Different methods

EXT. ROADSIDE PULL-OFF - FRIDAY - MORNING

Voss marks the moving tracker on a paper map. Reyes arrives in the grey estate with coffee; one lid is marked BLACK. Vale checks hers before drinking.

VOSS
You passed the track twice.

REYES
First established the road. Second checked the response.

VOSS
You may have created it.

Vale enlarges Reyes's dash-camera image: a narrow gap in the cottage shutter.

VALE
Someone could have watched. That's a sequence, not proof.

Voss assigns separate routes: Reyes stays local, Vale traces ownership, Voss follows the device. Nobody enters a confirmed site alone.

EXT./INT. VALLEY/RECORDS OFFICE/DEPOT - FRIDAY TO MONDAY

Reyes uses an ordinary builder's van, asks about dogs, buys eggs, gathers uncertain sightings. He waits outside the gate.

Vale joins old title entries to dissolved companies, a dead accountant and a correspondence address. She lays the pages in order.

VALE
An identity bridge. Enough to explain access. Not the man.

VOSS (PHONE)
Mac?

VALE
Not yet. His file begins after this name disappears.

SUNDAY: Vale joins Reyes. Together they inspect the cottage. Washed bowls, disturbed pantry dust, a chair below the shutter. In the barn, the hatchback and fresh tracker adhesive. Beyond the stream, tyre impressions from a different car.

REYES
What kind of driver keeps cars beside forgotten houses?

Vale looks at Mac's photograph rather than answering.

MONDAY: a depot manager watches Voss photograph and recover the tracker from a refrigerated trailer. Knife marks and the reconnected battery show deliberate transfer.

DEPOT MANAGER
Is it police?

VOSS
No.

DEPOT MANAGER
What is it then?

VOSS
A message.

INT. QUARRY CAFE - MONDAY - AFTERNOON

Tracker in an evidence bag. Title history beside it. Voss draws possible exits; none is a known destination.

REYES
He told us one man and a child.

VALE
The girl was probably watching the road.

REYES
She's thirteen. Not trained.

VALE
Mac's paperwork reduced her to cargo.

Voss draws a second circle beside the first.

VOSS
Elias isn't making every decision alone.

REYES
You're giving her too much credit.

VOSS
We gave her none. That was less accurate.

Reyes rubs his thumb against his coffee cup. Vale folds the identity chain. They have a better model, not the farmhouse.

# SEQ-06 — OBSERVATION, TRAP AND SAM

= SC024 — Market observation

INT. FARMHOUSE - TUESDAY - MORNING

An old bicycle stands in the yard, chain loose. Elias admits he left without a note to place the farm van at the livestock market under its old agricultural account.

ZOE
Next time, leave a note.

ELIAS
Yes.

On a newspaper photograph, everyone watches a politician except a man watching the exit.

ELIAS
Don't look at the threat. Look at what changes around it.

They agree on a conversational signal: THE FLOWERS ARE WRONG.

INT./EXT. MARKET TOWN - DAY

At the chemist Elias stays six metres away while Zoe chooses her own personal supplies. Schoolgirls recommend a cheaper shampoo. For a moment she has an ordinary choice. They buy food, yellow-striped socks and a plain blue notebook.

At a dark shop window, Zoe studies the reflected flower stall. A man buys lilies but watches the fish van's reflected square. A woman reads at the cafe, repaired scuff on her left heel. A bus blocks their view; she closes her book. A black saloon replaces the bus at the kerb.

ZOE
The flowers are wrong.

Elias keeps walking. She gives the sequence without looking back. He tests alternatives; she distinguishes what she saw from what she thinks it means.

ELIAS
Possibly the man from the cottage.

ZOE
You didn't see him.

ELIAS
You described him.

They finish the list. In another reflection Zoe sees the woman again, different bag, same repaired heel.

ZOE
Same book. Same shoes.

INT./EXT. CAR PARK/ROADS/LIVESTOCK MARKET - LATER

They leave the lift a floor early and walk up the ramp. At a faulty barrier the following car waits without impatience. Vehicles change during their circuit; Elias avoids the farmhouse road.

Voss receives sightings from Reyes and Vale, not a new tracker.

VALE (EARPIECE)
Detection confirmed. She signalled; he reacted.

At a reservoir dead end no tail enters. Back at the junction, the red van returns.

ELIAS
They're patient. We stop using the car they know.

At the livestock market they leave the estate under the auction building. In the cafe Zoe sees Vale questioning staff. A cattle lorry blocks the view as workers open a service gate.

ZOE
She watches the public exits. Nobody covers that one.

They collect the pre-positioned farm van with a key and an ordinary explanation. Shopping goes into crates; Zoe keeps her backpack. They leave with other white vans through the service gate.

EXT. LAY-BY - LATER

No repeated vehicle after further checks. Zoe holds the unwanted jar of honey.

ZOE
They may know it was me. You could tell me to stop.

ELIAS
Not seeing them wouldn't stop them seeing you. I trust what you notice.

INT. FARMHOUSE - NIGHT

They unpack. Yellow socks beside her notebook. Elias tastes the honey with exaggerated seriousness.

ELIAS
Average.

Zoe laughs, briefly. Then writes: TRUST IS ALSO A PATTERN.

= SC025 — A fallback and a bait room

EXT. LIVESTOCK MARKET - TUESDAY - AFTERNOON

Reyes closes the abandoned estate's boot too hard. Vale studies the service gate. Voss photographs the site plan.

REYES
She got lucky.

VALE
She saw the repaired heel after I changed my bag.

VOSS
Back to the identities. The car is meant to occupy us.

REYES
We had them.

VALE
We saw them.

INT./EXT. FARMHOUSE - WEDNESDAY TO SUNDAY

Zoe stalls the van between chalk lines. Elias reaches for the ignition; she catches his wrist.

ZOE
I can restart it.

He withdraws. She does. By morning's end she can reverse across the yard to block the rear entrance.

ELIAS
This isn't permission to drive on the road.

He leaves the van key in her hand.

THURSDAY: in her notebook she has written the surveillance sequence. Elias asks about the girls at the chemist.

ZOE
They don't matter to the route.

ELIAS
The route isn't the whole day. They didn't own every minute.

She adds the shampoo conversation.

FRIDAY: they walk the drainage ditch, stone shelter and route to the house with the blue gate. A cache holds water, cash, map and coat. Zoe tests what she would carry and leave.

SATURDAY: burned onions. The industrial map lies beside the stove.

ZOE
The lock-up. You're going to trap him.

ELIAS
Yes.

ZOE
He has a choice?

ELIAS
If he comes.

ZOE
He doesn't know what the choice is.

Elias sets a timer beside a travel clock. They revise a fallback together: he intends to return before two; if absent at four, her deadline is four ten. He leaves a written plan.

ZOE
Don't call it his choice if you make the whole room push him towards it.

SUNDAY NIGHT: Elias asks the contingency questions out of order. Zoe refuses any stranger claiming he sent them. The van blocks the gate; she leaves on foot, not along the road.

ZOE
Are you coming back?

ELIAS
I intend to. I can't promise.

ZOE
Whatever happens becomes part of the pattern.

ELIAS
I know.

He leaves by motorcycle. She places the notebook beside the timer.

INT. LOCK-UP - NIGHT

Camp bed, food, coat and warmth. An old nail above the bed, older damage beneath the staging. Elias checks the adjoining partition. Nothing from Zoe's belongings is used as bait.

= SC026 — Reyes enters

EXT. INDUSTRIAL ESTATE - SUNDAY - NIGHT

On Reyes's operational phone: POSSIBLE SITE. NO APPROACH UNTIL VERIFIED. Voss replies: HOLD. TWENTY-FIVE MINUTES.

Reyes studies Elias's photograph. DRIVER. He types VISUAL ONLY.

He checks the storage row and rear fence. Warm vent. Recent mud. Light beneath the lock-up door. His phone vibrates; he silences it. He forces the lock before backup arrives.

INT. LOCK-UP - CONTINUOUS

Empty camp bed. Coat. Boots. Reyes moves away from the doorway. Elias comes through the partition.

Reyes blocks him and drives him into the wall. Dust falls. Elias's shoulder takes the impact. The fight is difficult, brief and close.

REYES
You should have kept driving.

Beside the workbench Reyes loses his footing. The struggle ends on the concrete below the old nail. His breath stops. A train rattles the roof.

Elias kneels until it passes. Standing too fast, he grips the wall. He looks from the body to the exit, then at his hands. They shake. He lets them shake.

Reyes's phone lights: VOSS CALLING. The unlocked thread names Voss and Vale; markers show them approaching. Elias writes the identifiers on a page, powers the phone down and shields it in a tool pouch. Wallet and keys remain. He leaves through the adjoining unit.

INT./EXT. FARMHOUSE/LOCK-UP - LATE NIGHT

Zoe watches reflections, distinguishes rain on the shed from an approach, checks their loose-stone signal. The timer runs.

Voss stops at the open lock-up threshold. Calls Vale.

VOSS
Reyes is down. No. Not alive. The phone's gone.

Vale arrives. They document the scene. Her eye catches the packet of mints in Reyes's coat, then the old nail and opened partition.

VALE
The room didn't attack his competence.

VOSS
We should have pulled him after the market.

VALE
Mac gave us a description.

VOSS
And we didn't examine it.

INT. FARMHOUSE - EARLY MONDAY

Elias returns before Zoe's deadline. Torn shoulder, spreading bruise. She sits with the backpack on her lap. He places the shielded phone at the far end of the table.

ZOE
Did you kill him?

ELIAS
Yes.

ZOE
Was there another way?

ELIAS
Before tonight? Probably. Inside the room, no.

ZOE
You designed the room.

ELIAS
Yes.

She moves the backpack to the floor.

ZOE
You told me he had a choice.

ELIAS
I was wrong.

ZOE
That doesn't make you less wrong.

ELIAS
No.

His hand trembles once against the table.

ZOE
Are you frightened?

ELIAS
Of how quickly it became familiar.

She opens the notebook but doesn't write.

ZOE
Who did you teach before me? What was his name?

ELIAS
Not tonight. Tomorrow.

She neither assures him of trust nor leaves. Both stay in the dark kitchen with what he has said.

= SC027 — Sam

INT. FARMHOUSE - MONDAY - BEFORE DAWN

The forgotten timer rings at four. Elias silences it. His hand shakes as he fills the kettle. Zoe takes the kettle without touching him.

ZOE
You said tomorrow. His name first.

ELIAS
Sam. Samuel Mercer. Twenty-two when he died.

She sits. He tells it in pieces.

BRIEF MEMORY IMAGES, confined to his account: a saloon with one mismatched door; Sam spotting a man crossing twice with an unlit cigarette; eighteen months of remembered registrations and bad jokes.

ELIAS
He broke the instruction and warned me.

ZOE
Compromised the car and saved you. Both were true.

The images fragment: failing corridor light, chained exit, too many armed men for a brief naming two crews. Sam moves into the attack. Elias reaches him.

ELIAS
He told me where the car was. I said I was taking him with me.

ZOE
Did he believe you?

ELIAS
No. I'd taught him not to lie about the route.

The memory stops. Rain against the farmhouse window.

ZOE
Then what?

ELIAS
I finished the operation.

ZOE
Jackie's story said you destroyed both crews.

ELIAS
She didn't exaggerate. I used the work we'd done. Mac benefited. I didn't know the connection then. I still can't prove who meant what.

ZOE
You killed people who didn't kill Sam.

ELIAS
Yes.

ZOE
That isn't justice.

ELIAS
No.

He takes her half-full mug and empties it.

ZOE
You didn't ask. I was still drinking it.

He turns the mug upright and refills the kettle.

ELIAS
I stayed driving because I could follow instructions and call it harmless.

Zoe writes: SAMUEL MERCER. SAM. TWENTY-TWO.

ZOE
Loyalty isn't the same as fault. Stop talking like caring about you killed him.

ELIAS
He should have let it hit me.

ZOE
That's grief.

He starts to rise; shoulder pain stops him. He looks at the back door, then chooses to sit again.

ELIAS
Blame gave me a rule. Don't train anyone. Don't let anyone stand beside me.

ZOE
Punishment. I'm not Sam. You don't get to decide how far I'm allowed to learn because you see him.

Later, after sleep, Elias finds her watching the road. Her notebook remains closed.

ELIAS
I won't train you because you remind me of him. I won't refuse because you do, either.

She considers it.

ZOE
Almost sensible.

He lays the notes copied from Reyes's phone on the table without activating it. Together they mark probable roles, not certain biographies. She adds: SAM — BEFORE. NOT THE SAME.

# SEQ-07 — A MEETING THAT CANNOT TRANSFER ZOE

= SC028 — Contact

EXT. LOCK-UP AREA - MONDAY - MORNING

Police tape, uniforms and a storage manager. Vale and Voss watch from a distant car. Vale made the anonymous emergency call; Reyes's wallet and bracelet remain with him. His official identity can be found, while their removed contract materials conceal their own part.

INT. ROADSIDE CAFE - LATER

Four missed calls from Mac. Voss and Vale define their position before opening the handset.

VALE
Withdraw. We don't pursue the girl or help the next team.

MAC (PHONE)
Where is Reyes?

VOSS
Dead. Elias killed him in a prepared site. The brief was materially false. The contract is terminated.

MAC (PHONE)
Reyes ignored your order. That killed him.

VOSS
It exposed him. You tracked your driver before he disobeyed.

Mac threatens liability. Voss refuses the remaining fee. He tells Mac that Elias has Reyes's phone. The silence changes.

MAC (PHONE)
Change every channel.

VOSS
If he contacts us, we decide whether to answer.

The call ends. Neither calls withdrawal forgiveness or revenge.

INT. FARMHOUSE - AFTERNOON

Zoe looks at the proposed public meeting town.

ELIAS
You stay here.

ZOE
No. The contract is about me. I want to hear what they say when they know I can hear it.

He studies her, then opens the map again.

ELIAS
If I say move now?

ZOE
I move. If there's time, I ask why.

They agree on presence and immediate danger, not unconditional obedience. The folder and original drive stay with her.

EXT./INT. COMMUTER STATION - EVENING

Elias leaves a revised short note after Zoe rejects one exposing too much route detail. Away from the farmhouse and meeting town he briefly activates Reyes's handset on its remaining contact route.

MESSAGE: TUESDAY. 11:30. CLOCK SQUARE. NORTH CAFE. TWO ONLY.

He shuts it down. The dead handset does not return to the farmhouse.

INT. RENTED ROOM - EVENING

Vale's clean handset receives the invitation through the isolated incoming watch retained on Reyes's final route. Voss reads it.

VOSS
Public. Daylight. No separate approach to the girl. If he refuses, we leave.

Vale waits for the last answer.

VOSS
Yes. We leave.

She seals the handset away. Their watch can receive a message; it has not proved the forwarding intermediary safe.

= SC029 — Public terms

INT. NORTH CAFE, CLOCK SQUARE - TUESDAY - DAY

A waitress sets four drinks down in the wrong places. Voss swaps them. Zoe orders chips, no mayonnaise. Her backpack stays between her feet, beside Elias rather than across from him.

VOSS
Voss.

VALE
Vale.

ZOE
I know. Reyes.

Vale grips her cup.

VOSS
We're not here to avenge him. We terminated Mac's contract.

ELIAS
He agreed?

VOSS
No. We won't pursue Zoe, her folder or drive. He'll replace us.

Elias explains buyer, false family destination and Cain's separate interest. Zoe answers questions about her own file herself. Jackie stays unnamed; coercion remains their inference.

VALE
We've seen this delegated language on recoveries. Some real. We didn't know enough to separate the others.

ZOE
That's useful.

VALE
Culpable.

Voss slides a marked intermediary sheet beneath the newspaper: payments, number forwarding, vehicle supplier and certifying solicitor. Meridian appears in Zoe's folder. Verified entries are marked, not assumed universal.

VOSS
The next brief makes him the abductor, you coerced or unstable, the drive stolen property.

Zoe sets down a chip.

VOSS
We need to know you're here by choice.

VALE
You and me at the counter. Elias visible. Thirty seconds.

ZOE
No. You decide which room makes me truthful. Everyone who decided something about me first moved me away from the person who might disagree.

VOSS
Would he let you leave?

ZOE
He doesn't own the answer. He stops me when danger is immediate. He explains when it isn't. Sometimes he gets it wrong.

ELIAS
Frequently.

VALE
We remain here.

Voss nods. The meeting continues at the same table.

Vale notices the fruit seller looking beyond a customer. New men near the flowers and pharmacy. A coat folded over an arm in clear weather. She doesn't turn twice.

VALE
Voss. Two confirmed. Not ours.

Elias flattens his hand on the newspaper. Zoe stops chewing.

VALE
The incoming watch. Number forwarding.

The intermediary sheet lies between them. Their invitation has reached somebody else.

= SC030 — The delivery van

INT./EXT. NORTH CAFE/KITCHEN YARD - CONTINUOUS

A bread van reverses into the service lane. Zoe watches the driver bring trays inside.

ZOE
That route. We're not leaving separately.

Vale confirms the road behind the post office. Voss chooses to stay and obstruct the watchers. Vale spills coffee onto a man's shirt; the room reacts. Elias and Zoe reach the kitchen.

The stained-shirt man follows, raincoat over his right forearm.

ZOE
He has a weapon.

The delivery driver sees its shape and throws Elias the keys.

DRIVER
Take it. Police.

He retreats behind trays with his phone. Zoe enters the passenger seat. As the pistol appears, Vale knocks the gun arm against the wall. A shot strikes concrete. Voss blocks another man at the kitchen door.

Vale moves aside, letting the van leave.

VALE
Left after the gate. Right at the second bridge.

Zoe looks back once. A decision, not a promise.

EXT. DISTRIBUTION ROAD/FOOTPATH/BUS - LATER

A blue hatchback stays with them. They cannot approach the stored fallback car while followed.

Zoe sees a turning lorry offer a gap into a distribution site. Elias takes it. The van reaches a staff-road dead end. They abandon it, join workers walking out and leave by a pedestrian gate.

The man following doesn't belong to the workers' rhythm; security notices him. He calls Zoe's name. Elias doesn't seize her. She keeps moving.

They board a bus and leave it before the final stop.

EXT./INT. GARAGE/BLACK HATCHBACK - LATER

On the walk, Zoe refuses help with the heavy backpack, then chooses to hand it over. Elias carries it on his uninjured shoulder. At the garage he returns it before taking the key.

She sits beside him in the black hatchback. They drive away from the farmhouse.

INT./EXT. SIDE STREET/COAST ROAD - AFTERNOON

Voss and Vale regroup as sirens reach the square.

VOSS
It doesn't erase Reyes.

VALE
No. It tells us what the truce requires.

In the car Zoe tests the same limit.

ZOE
They helped.

ELIAS
That counts as today.

ZOE
What now?

ELIAS
An offline machine. Verify the names. Stop using refuges as the plan.

ZOE
We're still running.

ELIAS
The direction changed.

He turns inland. Zoe steadies the backpack with one foot.

= SC031 — The index

INT. HOTEL ROOM - TUESDAY - EVENING

Two beds. A used laptop, write blocker and two sealed blank drives. Elias removes the wireless card and sets it where Zoe can see it. They test the blocker before her archive comes near it.

ZOE
Explain first. Twice.

ELIAS
The original stays untouched. Work from a copy.

Only then does she unwrap the drive. He waits.

ZOE
You can pick it up.

He connects it through the blocker. A long copy estimate appears. They eat and read her paperback while it runs. This time he wakes her when the copy is ready.

The original returns to her backpack before the working copy is opened. Most of the volume remains encrypted. A small catalogue opens: numbers, dates, routes, accounts, actions.

Zoe searches her folder number: A17-04221. Its placement links to P06-11803 and AC019. She compares the paper payment code.

ZOE
Mac. No. Same code isn't the same as his name.

She writes: AC019 — PROBABLY MAC. PROVE IT.

Another action: MONITOR. ACTIVE. Years before the abduction.

Next: RECOVER. Monday before the van.

Next: EXCEPTION. Friday. UNRESOLVED.

ZOE
They called taking me recovery. What changed on Monday?

Her account-code search yields sixty-three actions. Grouped by roots, twenty-three numbers remain.

ZOE
Twenty-three children— no. Numbers.

A DECEASED status stops her hand. Elias cannot say whether it describes person or case. She moves back to her own row without inventing a face for the number.

ELIAS
Enough. Tell me what you need to ask next.

She looks at the hundreds of rows, then writes a rule.

ZOE
Don't use the kids to prove what the adults did. Names, addresses, where they are — no. Use adults. Money. Signatures. Companies.

She places a question mark beside ROUTES.

ELIAS
Don't publish routes until we know what they point to.

They leave the encrypted volume closed. One working copy stays with Elias. A second goes into a padded envelope. Both reject proposed storage tied to an old identity; they agree on a retrievable independent location.

Her original, folder and ordinary paperback fit together in the backpack. The notebook stays beside the bed.

# SEQ-08 — A NARROW QUESTION BEATS A LARGE ACCUSATION

= SC032 — Routes and exceptions

INT. HOTEL/LIBRARY/DOCUMENT BOX - WEDNESDAY

Zoe confronts Elias about another unannounced route check. He admits it was a decision to leave. They deposit the sealed second copy in a locked document box at a print shop; both retain its retrieval code.

At the library the archive stays packed. Zoe compares N4-7 and E2-4 from her notes: broad placements, returns and ordinary closures versus narrower recoveries, transfers and holds.

ZOE
Same route doesn't mean same thing. Find something outside the drive.

She crosses out ROUTE DOES NOT EQUAL CRIME as too neat.

INT. VOSS AND VALE'S ROOM - WEDNESDAY/THURSDAY

Voss compares old paper files: genuine witness relocation, a lawful return to a grandmother, and children moved after the client changed to Meridian.

VALE
We verified the paper against the number printed on the paper.

VOSS
Not an independent check.

He calls a court through its public switchboard, gives no child's name. The supposed order has no matching record; the named judge wasn't sitting family cases there that year. Further checks rule out a simple transfer.

VALE
I remember the girl. Yellow coat.

They do not contact her mother; they don't know who is still monitored.

INT. STUDY ROOM - THURSDAY - DAY

The working copy opens without wireless access. On a whiteboard: NORMAL / OVERRIDE? / WHY.

Zoe's transition history narrows to seven rows. MONITOR to REVIEW from an external source, then REVIEW to RECOVER: OVR3 / MAN.

ZOE
A manual action. But manual doesn't prove bad.

They inspect comparison histories: ordinary returns, permanent placements and a manual transfer without override. Four override classes appear. Zoe totals 486 actions, writes NOT 486 KIDS.

Review followed by override narrows most subsequent choices to RECOVER, TRANSFER or HOLD. Ordinary reviews spread across many outcomes.

ELIAS
That's what the visible sample suggests.

A lookup supplies labels. OVR3: PROTECTIVE RECOVERY AUTHORITY. OVR4: EXECUTIVE CONTAINMENT AUTHORITY. BRG: RESTRICTED PLACEMENT BRIDGE.

ZOE
Children aren't spills.

No BRG appears in her current root. They don't call that an acquittal. A linked predecessor changes the screen: REVIEW to BRG under OVR2, then PLACE, P06-11803.

Helen and Brian's placement code.

Zoe pushes her chair back. Elias touches nothing.

ZOE
Mine started in the normal part.

She corrects her own wording: ORDINARY-LOOKING.

ZOE
My life turned on an override.

At the other table, Vale lays genuine intake information beside the false later authority.

VALE
Current information. Either someone got in or someone inside helped.

Back in the study room, Zoe erases the board, including a faint SHADOW. Outside she asks whether her original family was unsafe.

ELIAS
We can't infer that. Something was recorded. We don't know how accurate or lawful it was.

She folds separate KNOW and DON'T KNOW pages. The next question: WHO WAS ON THE OTHER SIDE?

= SC033 — Recipients

INT. RENTED OFFICE - FRIDAY - DAY

The new beige estate's keys lie beside an isolated laptop. Original drive under Zoe's chair; working copy on the desk.

Zoe reads WHO RECEIVES A BRG CASE? She moves her hands off the keyboard.

ZOE
Not mine. Another one.

A different root links to RCP-0447. The lookup: RECEIVING CANDIDATE PROFILE.

Stable home, finance and tenure. Then exposure, disclosure, compliance, attachment, leverage, sponsor. Zoe's expression changes.

ZOE
They want good parents.

ELIAS
Stable households. Those aren't the same thing. Chaos draws attention.

A declined profile has high professional exposure and low compliance. Another household is APPROVED — NOT MATCHED, without a child attached. RCP-0447 specifies capacity one, age six to nine, permanent, before its case link. A completed match clears a numeric threshold.

ZOE
They profile the adults too. Being here doesn't prove what they knew.

She writes: FIND OUT WHAT THEY WERE TOLD.

INT. VOSS AND VALE'S ROOM - SAME DAY

Vale remembers a normal house, family photographs and a woman kneeling to the arriving girl and crying.

VALE
I thought relief. It isn't evidence of what she knew.

They trace adult property and sponsor records, not the child's school, medical history or public identity.

INT. RENTED OFFICE - LATER

Motive codes have no readable definitions. They leave them unresolved. A match-summary database might contain private child records.

ZOE
Don't open the rows. Fields only.

Schema columns: age fit, sex fit, physical characteristics, behaviour, medical, history, preference, attachment, score.

She snaps the laptop closed and walks around the table.

ZOE
They shop. And call it safeguarding.

ELIAS
What do you want to do?

ZOE
Stop. For today. Tomorrow we don't open those rows either.

He nods until she tells him to stop nodding. A brief laugh breaks through.

INT. BEIGE ESTATE/INN - LATER

Cake boxes between them. Zoe thinks of Helen cutting labels from shirts, Brian at a school play — no verdict attached to these ordinary memories.

ZOE
They'd score well. High attachment.

ELIAS
Nothing there tells us what they knew.

She adds a new rule: PRIVATE ISN'T AUTOMATICALLY EVIDENCE.

They list compartments, real infrastructure and convincing paper.

ELIAS
People assuming somebody else checked.

She underlines it. Then closes the notebook.

ZOE
Years of monitor. What woke my case up on Monday?

= SC034 — The limited query

INT. SELF-STORAGE MEETING ROOM - SATURDAY - DAY

Boxes pass the glass wall. Elias closes the blind; the archive laptop remains offline.

ELIAS
Sometimes being seen is safer than disappearing.

Zoe filters external events. Most are ordinary updates. Her own query audit gives Q-882104, LEGACY PLACEMENT VERIFICATION, CE/41/771, target P06-11803. Monday 09:14. Supporting basis: SEIZED MATERIAL REVIEW.

ZOE
They asked about the placement. Not directly about me.

A silent integrity-watch alert follows at 09:17. Restricted review, escalated risk, protective recovery authority approved. At 10:21: RECOVER — OPEN.

ZOE
Sixty-seven minutes. I didn't do something to cause Monday. Someone asked a question.

ELIAS
The timing strongly links it. We don't know the intended end state.

At 11:03 the ordinary answer says RECORD UNAVAILABLE — ARCHIVE MIGRATION. A follow-up asks for document authority. It is held, deferred and barred from release. No final answer appears in the snapshot.

ZOE
Someone was still looking then. Not necessarily now.

At Voss's separate desk, an old lawful adult job suggests CE can mean Central Evidence Liaison. He marks it as a possible reference family, not an identification of Zoe's questioner.

Back on Zoe's screen: sponsor AC019 voice advisory, acknowledged 16:48. She remembers Helen's later call.

ZOE
Maybe connected. No transcript. No proof she knew about the van.

She writes the limit beside it. Surveillance active 18:06. Recovery window Tuesday 15:00–18:00.

Her breath catches. She closes the table.

ZOE
The question made them move.

EXT. RESERVOIR - LATER

A dog refuses to give back its ball. Zoe writes ASK / WATCH / DON'T GUESS.

ZOE
One fact. No child name, medical detail or location. But know who the question could hurt.

ELIAS
And a source we can test. Not the whole drive sent to somebody we can't identify.

INT. UNDERGROUND CAR PARK - SAME AFTERNOON

Mac hears from his solicitor: the official follow-up still exists. The archive isn't contained. He selects a contact he normally would not call, pauses, then presses dial.

= SC035 — Circular answers

INT. GARDEN-CENTRE CAFE - SUNDAY - MORNING

Zoe places three cards: ASK / WATCH / DON'T GUESS. She chooses Meridian's document certification, not her own case.

ZOE
It joins my folder and Voss's jobs without my name. One reference. Watch what changes.

ELIAS
A normal comparison too.

A brief fragment reaches Voss's passive route: MPS-17 / 14:00. No refuge or meeting location. Vale finds the old reference family and prepares a known legitimate control.

INT. PUBLIC LIBRARY - AFTERNOON

Archive laptop packed away. On a public terminal Zoe asks only whether MPS-17-4408 is an authenticated-copy reference. No child identity. At two she submits, logs out and moves when Elias asks.

Automated intake is not an answer.

INT. VOSS AND VALE'S WORKROOM - INTERCUT

Voss calls Meridian's public number for MPS-09-2281, a known legitimate adult file. Routine confirmation. For MPS-17-4408 the clerk stops, requests his name and sends him to compliance. The line disconnects.

Vale writes: SAME QUESTION / DIFFERENT PATH.

Then an old contractor route receives: HISTORIC CERTIFICATION MATERIAL — DO NOT RELEASE.

VOSS
Send nothing back. Now watch.

INT./EXT. LEGAL OFFICE/CAIN'S HOUSE/MAC'S STUDY - AFTERNOON

The restricted-family alert spreads to legal, document-control and sponsor compartments. Mac's solicitor demands who else received it. He cannot stop the automatic distribution.

Cain orders restricted paper copies moved. Mac calls to stop him. Neither accepts the other's responsibility for the missing archive.

CAIN
Then stop people asking questions.

INT. INTERNET CAFE - LATER

Meridian requests Zoe's full identity instead of answering. She does not reply. A temporary handset receives only: CONTROL CLEARED NORMALLY. TEST FLAGGED. CHANNEL WARNING ISSUED.

ZOE
It proves different treatment and wider reaction. Not forgery. Not that every records clerk is criminal.

She records that Voss and Vale asked for no more data.

ELIAS
Evidence towards trust.

ZOE
No more questions yet. Five tells them we're mapping them.

They retire the temporary channel. The archive remains closed all day.

At the motel a car door wakes Zoe. She checks: family, luggage, children. Her heart settles slowly.

ZOE
Fear's faster than evidence.

She returns to the cards. NEXT: FIND WHAT THE FALSE ORDER COPIED.

= SC036 — Two orders

INT. VOSS AND VALE'S ROOM - MONDAY - MORNING

Two orders beneath the lamp. A historic contractor copy and an authenticated court copy obtained through an independent solicitor.

Same case, hearing, judge and seal format. Different operative text.

Vale reads the true order: supervised contact continues; temporary placement continues; review in six weeks.

The variant suspends contact, transfers authority and adds a paragraph permitting recovery, transport and relocation. The true order has no paragraph thirteen.

VALE
We verified the wrapper. Not the text.

VOSS
Yes.

VALE
Someone had the real one.

VOSS
Access to a genuine copy. Not proof which person altered it.

INT. DOCUMENT-REVIEW ROOM - SAME DAY

Zoe searches MPS-17-4408 on the working copy. It links another root, H09-55216, to document control: BASE FC-771/12, VARIANT S3, OVR2, VERIFY C2, DELIVERY MPS.

They inspect metadata, not the private source image. Source image first, text extract and seal map, then the variant. Earlier versions remain unknown.

Zoe sends only BASE FC-771/12 / COMPARE TEXT. Voss returns only the operative differences, no child names.

ZOE
The override changes what happens. This makes it look as if a judge ordered it.

C2 reads CONTROLLED VERIFICATION: supplied primary and secondary contacts, escalation back to document control, external registry check discouraged.

At the other table Vale calls the historic supplied line. It still says AUTHENTICATED, but will not name the source court or answer about paragraph thirteen.

The independent source says otherwise.

ZOE
Different doors. Same answer machine.

She draws REAL COURT outside the loop.

EXT. CAFE - AFTERNOON

Over food Zoe lists what they cannot prove: creator, source supplier, knowing Meridian staff, court insider, recipient knowledge, or the same mechanism in her case.

ZOE
Seeing the fake doesn't mean you knew it was fake. No more on that child or her mother.

INT. MAC'S STUDY/CAIN'S HOUSE - INTERCUT

Mac wants the verification line shut. His solicitor warns that closure after comparison exposes significance; the legacy system does not answer to one sponsor.

Cain orders no further box movement. He watches access records and waits for the next question.

INT. HOTEL - NIGHT

Zoe looks at her backpack, then away.

ZOE
Not my order yet. I want the next move to show the system, not just explain me.

She writes THE PROFILE. The real years of her childhood remain real even if their starting authority was false.

# SEQ-09 — THE HOUSEHOLD AND THE INDEPENDENT CONTRADICTION

= SC037 — A profile is not a person

INT. HOTEL ROOM - TUESDAY - MORNING

A family-image thumbnail waits beside RCP-0447. Zoe closes it.

ZOE
Could be children. We don't need the photograph.

She opens only the motive key: child loss, inability to have another child, failed lawful adoption. A prior-child anchor enters the match calculation. Strong physical correspondence and predicted attachment carry high weights.

ZOE
They measured how much this child would remind them of the one who died.

ELIAS
That's what these fields appear to say.

ZOE
That doesn't make it fate.

Dates: death years earlier, unsuccessful treatment, lawful application refused, sponsor K08 four months later, failed matches, eventual acceptance. The source of sponsor knowledge remains unknown.

INT. VOSS AND VALE'S ROOM - INTERCUT

Adult public records independently confirm child loss, not every private motive. Vale closes the newspaper photograph. The old receiving sheet says PRIOR VIEWING COMPLETED and RECIPIENT APPROVED.

Signed acknowledgement: standard placement and adoption processes not applicable; questions through sponsor only. Private administration payment. Partial knowledge, not proof they knew the child was stolen.

INT./EXT. HOTEL/CANAL - LATER

Zoe receives their bounded report. The archive's D2 category is supported by conduct outside it, not treated as a verdict itself.

ZOE
Something terrible, then something real grows out of it. Proving the first can hurt the second.

ELIAS
Yes.

She makes two lists: EXPOSE adult authorisations, payments and knowing choices; PROTECT children, current homes, legitimate placements and private grief proving nothing.

AC019 links seven recipient profiles. She stops her hand.

ZOE
Profiles. Not seven children. No more private rows.

At night she draws DOCUMENT / MONEY / LEGAL. Separate small questions, no child data. A next test, not permission to consume every answer.

= SC038 — Reaction

INT. HOTEL/CONTRACTOR OFFICE - WEDNESDAY - MORNING

Three plain envelopes: DOCUMENT, MONEY, LEGAL. Zoe records normal answers and absence of visible response before the test.

Meridian's general certification-category enquiry answers routinely. She writes NORMAL. Boring is useful.

Voss receives an old adult accounting reference and asks only client money or service revenue. The clerk answers, then asks about retained invoices and scans.

Vale separately asks who issued an old legal-verification reference. They do not combine the prompts before observing the answers.

At the contractor portal: £2,400 AVAILABLE ON ACCEPTANCE. HISTORIC FILE RECONCILIATION. CONFIRM DESTRUCTION OF RETAINED COPIES.

They photograph amount, time and condition. No acceptance or reply. Six minutes later the offer disappears.

VALE
They want us to say we destroyed the files.

VOSS
Record what happened.

EXT. RECORDS SITE - AFTERNOON

Vale watches from an ordinary cafe as an identifiable Meridian compliance participant and a legal administrator meet at the same site. Boxes move. She can report who arrived and left, not what they said.

INT. HOTEL - LATER

Zoe logs the independent reports. The offline drive has no magic live update. She uses its earlier histories to find a recurring HOUSEHOLD STABILITY CONFIRMATION after restricted activity.

Her old query sequence links the check to P06-11803.

ZOE
Helen's call before the van. Maybe that's what it was checking.

ELIAS
A possible connection. We don't have its words.

She writes that limit.

INT. MAC'S STUDY - SAME DAY

Cain names the quiet placement now attracting questions: P06-11803. Mac sees Helen, Brian and Zoe behind the code. He tells his side not to move further material; every protection choice can expose a link.

INT. HOTEL - NIGHT

Zoe leaves motive and image fields closed.

ZOE
Tomorrow, what they knew. What they agreed to. Not whether they loved me. I know they did.

She puts the unopened original beside her bed.

= SC039 — Helen and Brian's file

INT. ROOM ABOVE A TYRE SHOP - THURSDAY - DAY

P06-11803 fills the screen. Zoe holds her hand away from the trackpad.

ZOE
No motives, photographs, medical details or match factors. What they knew, agreed to, did.

RCP-0193 existed before she was attached. AC019 sponsored it. D3 — KNOWING SHADOW PARTICIPANT.

ELIAS
The label isn't a verdict. Look beneath it.

A withdrawal. Nine days closed. Mac contact. Four days later reopened. Confidential handling accepted before any child match.

ZOE
They didn't say yes to me. They said yes to this.

Signed terms: ordinary adoption process not applicable; questions through sponsor, not normal channels. Four acknowledgements. Introduction, assessment, preparation, documents, transfer and support payments: Brian, joint account, one Mac advance reimbursed. Five years of continuing support payments.

ZOE
No line says price of child. It still says what they paid for.

Reports ask about attachment, early-identity questions and outside contacts. Match score 97. Visual introduction recorded, three hours before acceptance. She leaves the reasons and images closed.

INT. HELEN AND BRIAN'S KITCHEN - INTERCUT

Withheld calls. A message requests stability through the sponsor channel. Brian wants to ask Mac.

HELEN
Calling them might help whoever took her.

BRIAN
We don't know that.

HELEN
We don't know it won't.

He looks upstairs, towards Zoe's empty room. Helen puts the phone in a drawer.

INT. TYRE-SHOP ROOM - LATER

Voss's separate ledger corroborates AC019 recipient sponsorship, confidential-placement support and later recovery work. Mac is a broker into a door, not owner of the whole architecture.

Zoe looks at her notes.

ZOE
They loved me. It doesn't change what they signed. It doesn't mean they knew every false order or whose child I was.

ELIAS
No.

She disconnects the working copy herself. They leave for food.

Later she writes four questions: WHY REOPEN? WHY 97? WHAT HAPPENED IN THREE HOURS? WHAT WOULD THEY CHOOSE NOW?

She closes the notebook. Those answers can wait; the boundary of the real institution comes next.

= SC040 — The real organisation

INT. MEETING ROOM - FRIDAY - DAY

Pop music rises each time the warehouse door below opens. Zoe tests Westmere, not a whole institution-shaped villain.

A sample ends RETURN HOME, then three months of support and closure. Another permanent placement has an outside solicitor and formal panel. No BRG, sponsor or controlled document route.

Three headings: ORDINARY / SUSPICIOUS / UNKNOWN.

ZOE
Unknown isn't suspicious written politely. If we release everything, a lawful family could read its route as proof it was stolen.

ELIAS
And the real contradiction gets buried.

She writes NO WHOLE-DRIVE DUMP.

INT. VOSS AND VALE'S ROOM - INTERCUT

A lawful reunification file. Vale remembers a father crying before his child reaches the door. Reyes carries the broken suitcase, then looks away from the embrace.

Voss calls a retired transport coordinator through an ordinary route. She remembers messy, real work: councils or courts decided; Westmere arranged placement and transport. It could not issue removal powers. She knew no BRG. An unknown caller recently asked about retained files and that term.

They forward a narrow account without her identity or private child detail.

INT. MEETING ROOM - LATER

Public descriptions and outside legal information set a limit around Westmere's power. Zoe draws the lawful work in black. Red lines cut through selected permissions, documents and sponsor routes; they don't cover the page.

ZOE
Not another whole organisation. Something using the real one.

ELIAS
Then some workers may become witnesses.

ZOE
Some will defend what they know was good. We shouldn't lie about that to prove what wasn't.

She closes the sample, leaves the innocent identities untouched.

At night she writes: FIND SOMEONE OUTSIDE THE LOOP. SHOW WHAT CARRIES THEIR AUTHORITY.

ZOE
Is this yours?

The question is smaller than the archive. It can be answered.

= SC041 — North Vale answers

INT. SERVICE CAFE/PRIYA'S OFFICE - SATURDAY - MORNING

On the table: a neutral question, rewritten until accusations are gone. Case H09-55216, not Zoe or the yellow-coat girl. Base NV-CF/4419; disputed supplement NV-CF/4419-S.

PRIYA DEY reads the redacted excerpt. Independent of Mac's chain, she knows a historic contractor audit yielded materially different authorities. No drive or Zoe location.

REQUEST: Did North Vale issue this supplemental authority on that date, granting recovery, transport or relocation powers?

She sends it to North Vale's current published legal-services contact.

INT. NORTH VALE OFFICES - LATER

Records staff find the base and use their own internal directory, not the disputed number. The retired file lawyer is consulted. The supplement is absent and the claimed powers inconsistent with the department's authority.

INT. CAFE - MIDDAY

A phone summary arrives. Zoe restrains her first response.

ZOE
Priya says they said no. Wait for writing.

Later, a redacted written response confirms the base reference, no supplement record, no issued instrument conferring those powers. It cannot determine whether the passage was altered, created elsewhere or falsely attributed.

Zoe reads that last limit twice.

North Vale retains its sent record. Priya the original reply. Voss and the pair hold separate redacted copies, apart from the archive.

INT. NORTH VALE - AFTERNOON

The department opens its own incident: possible unauthorised representation of legal powers. It exists without knowledge of Zoe.

INT. VOSS AND VALE'S ROOM - SAME DAY

Vale aligns dispatch timestamps. Provisional transport before authority could be ordinary. A specific permanent receiving destination was already confirmed before the claimed supplement arrived.

VALE
The destination waited for the order.

INT. CAFE - LATER

Zoe writes DECISION FIRST, then corrects it at Elias's question.

ZOE
Destination first. Authority later. That's what we can prove.

INT. MAC'S STUDY/CAIN'S OFFICE - INTERCUT

Both hear the purported authority has failed outside verification. Mac considers pressure, then stops it. North Vale's present legal and governance records are beyond his old verification loop.

INT. HOTEL - NIGHT

The written contradiction is stored separately from the drive.

ZOE
One authority, one case. Not everything. But this record doesn't disappear if my drive does.

She leaves her own order closed. Next: what the contractors did when they believed such paper.

= SC042 — The yellow coat

INT. CONTRACTOR WORKROOM - SUNDAY MORNING

Voss places two transport files side by side. Vale recognises the child before he gives the historic identifier.

VALE
Yellow coat.

The first file: WESTMERE. Temporary placement. Maternal contact continuing. A review ahead.

MEMORY — A SERVICE STATION, YEARS EARLIER

A girl shivers in a coat too thin for the weather. Reyes puts his own coat around her. A paper cup disappears between oversized sleeves.

REYES
Your teeth are doing the paperwork for you.

BACK IN THE WORKROOM

Vale touches a line: REQUESTED MOTHER REPEATEDLY.

VOSS
Real authority. That journey.

Eleven weeks later: MERIDIAN. Contact suspended. Private receiving household. A different order.

Dispatch Friday. Authority Monday. Voss puts a finger on ACTIVATION PENDING AUTHORITY.

VALE
The house was waiting.

VOSS
A destination before an order can be legitimate. We prove what this one did.

Property-service records: child-specific clothes and a room prepared forty-eight hours before the disputed authority. A premium tied to avoiding external escalation. Meridian's contractor onboarding, supported by AC019.

A dispatcher answers on speaker. Jobs were released when the attached authority arrived; somebody else was assumed to have checked the client.

Voss opens his own debrief. CHILD RESISTANT AT INITIAL CONTACT.

MEMORY — A TEMPORARY CARER'S HALLWAY

The girl stands behind the carer. Voss holds out the order. Vale waits by the door.

CARER
Her mother's finished treatment. There's a review coming.

Voss indicates the verification number printed on the document. The carer calls. Listens. Returns the order, her certainty gone.

GIRL
Does Mum know?

VOSS
It's arranged.

BACK IN THE WORKROOM

Voss does not look away from the line he wrote years ago.

VOSS
I didn't know that.

He types what he remembers: the challenge, the supplied number, the confirmation, his answer to the child. Separates it from what the retained records establish.

VALE
She did everything right.

VOSS
And we treated the answer as enough.

Vale reaches towards the search field. Stops before entering the girl's name.

VALE
She'd be grown now.

VOSS
We don't need her current life to prove our job.

She closes the search. They preserve the two files together.

INT. GUESTHOUSE - SUNDAY EVENING

Zoe reads their bounded account through the relay. The drive stays closed.

ZOE
Someone knew the household. Someone supplied the order. He told her it was arranged.

ELIAS
He says he did.

ZOE
At least he put himself in it.

She writes one sentence, then puts down the notebook. Elias checks the lock.

ZOE
Tomorrow?

ELIAS
North Vale may ask Westmere directly.

ZOE
Good.

The first journey's request for her mother remains beside the second journey's word: RESISTANT.

# SEQ-10 — THROUGH GLASS; JACKIE REACHES OUT

= SC043 — A record beyond the drive

INT. WESTMERE COMPLIANCE OFFICE - MONDAY MORNING

08:07. Elaine Foster receives North Vale's preservation request. Do not destroy, amend or migrate specified historic records. She opens H09-55216.

Migrated summary: protected transfer, permanent placement, closed.

Older material: maternal contact, reunification work, another review due. Two days later an external authority changed the case.

Elaine calls Martin Hale, retired compliance administrator.

MARTIN (PHONE)
We sent things to an extension. Somebody called back. Status changed.

ELAINE
Did you contact the issuing authority?

MARTIN (PHONE)
That wasn't the process.

His notebook contains changing extensions and procedures. No child records.

ELAINE
Keep it. Don't give it to anyone yet.

She checks a small sample. A lawful return home. A lawful permanent placement. Then another case routed through the suspect legal service.

Her incident note distinguishes legitimate restricted confidentiality from a subset whose independent authority cannot yet be verified.

INT. WESTMERE ARCHIVE DESK - LATE MORNING

Seven boxes wait on a trolley. A polite courier presents an urgent legal reconciliation order issued today, after the hold.

COURIER
I'm collecting what I've been told to collect.

ELAINE
I understand. These aren't being released.

He asks about scans. Photographs of labels. She refuses both. Checks the authorising office against Martin's old service range.

She signs: RELEASE REFUSED — PRESERVATION HOLD ACTIVE. 11:48.

COURIER
Am I in trouble?

ELAINE
As far as I know, no.

He leaves with his refusal copy. Elaine attaches the collection order to the incident.

INT. CONTRACTOR WORKROOM - DAY

Priya asks for a supported account of how a contractor verified an order.

VOSS
Written. One case. Properly redacted.

VALE
They're asking the right question.

INT. HELEN AND BRIAN'S HOUSE - DAY

Helen calls Mac. Brian watches from the kitchen doorway.

HELEN
You kept telling us it was handled. Did you tell them where she was?

MAC (PHONE)
I told them the placement was stable.

HELEN
She has a name. Use it.

A pause.

MAC (PHONE)
Zoe.

HELEN
If you call again, I'm not answering.

She ends the call. No triumphant relief.

INT. WESTMERE COMPLIANCE OFFICE - AFTERNOON

Elaine speaks directly with North Vale and both organisations' current legal advisers. No Meridian intermediary.

NORTH VALE LAWYER (SPEAKER)
The base reference is real. We cannot support the supplemental powers. Could Westmere delegate recovery powers to a private sponsor?

ELAINE
No.

NORTH VALE LAWYER (SPEAKER)
Then what authorised the move?

ELAINE
I don't know.

EXT. RESERVOIR CAR PARK - AFTERNOON

Zoe reads the logged box refusal. A dog ignores its owner's thrown ball.

ZOE
They wrote it down themselves.

ELIAS
Yes.

ZOE
We can't control what they ask next.

ELIAS
No.

The backpack is at her feet. For the first time, it is one record among several.

ZOE
What do we control?

ELIAS
What we disclose. Whether we stay here.

She checks the time and agrees to move on.

= SC044 — Cost of containment

INT. LEGAL-ADMINISTRATION BACK OFFICE - TUESDAY MORNING

Mac, Cain, restricted legal and Margaret Shaw sit around a table none of them wanted to share.

Margaret lays out a list: North Vale incident. Westmere incident. Priya's correspondence. Contractor copies. Former staff.

CAIN
They incriminate themselves too.

MARGARET
That doesn't make their records disappear.

Cain insists they cannot walk away after the cost. Margaret closes the folder.

MARGARET
Reyes is dead whether you find her or not.

Mac looks at the empty space beyond Cain's shoulder.

MAC
Pressure creates another witness now.

Cain's glare does not change the list.

CAIN
Publish that archive and children get exposed. Real families. Real placements.

MARGARET
True. Separate criminal evidence from protected material.

CAIN
Who decides?

MARGARET
Not us.

His truthful warning does not entitle him to recover the drive.

INT. LAUNDERETTE - MORNING

The backpack sits between Elias's feet. Zoe watches shirts tumble. A boy declares an empty basket a pirate ship.

ZOE
You lost the cottage. The work. Sleep. Why stay?

Elias lets a shirt rise and fall before answering.

ELIAS
Leaving wouldn't undo what I know.

Zoe considers that. He does not offer a tally of sacrifice or ask her to repay it.

ZOE
You're losing a sock as well.

He looks into the drum. She was right.

INT. ZOE'S BEDROOM, HELEN AND BRIAN'S HOUSE - AFTERNOON

Helen holds a grey jumper. The room contains five years of Zoe's choices.

HELEN
We kept paying. Reporting. What did it cost us?

BRIAN
We got her.

He hears the words after he says them.

BRIAN
I didn't mean—

HELEN
We got to keep her. What did she lose?

Brian sits beside her. Neither touches the jumper.

HELEN
If they call, I'm done. Not another report.

BRIAN
What if she finds out?

HELEN
You know her.

The room offers no defence, only memories.

INT. CONTRACTOR WORKROOM - AFTERNOON

An offer on Voss's laptop: favourable good-faith characterisation if retained material is surrendered. Vale reads it twice.

VALE
Priya keeps her copy.

VOSS
Yes.

VALE
If you save yourself, you don't get to decide for everyone else.

He forwards the offer as a possible inducement. Deletes nothing. Accepts nothing.

INT. GUESTHOUSE - EVENING

Zoe receives the narrow report. The drive remains closed.

ZOE
They want the next choice to make the last one right.

ELIAS
It can't.

She writes the household reference in her notebook. Beneath it: WHY THEM? WHY ME? WHY 97?

The boy in the laundry basket has already gone home. The washing lies folded between them.

ZOE
Tomorrow. Enough to answer that. Not everything.

Elias leaves the notebook with her.

= SC045 — The match

INT. GUESTHOUSE OUTSIDE HEREFORD - WEDNESDAY MORNING

Zoe sits at the archive laptop. The original drive stays in her bag; the protected working copy supplies RCP-0193.

ZOE
No letters. No photograph. Only what explains the decision.

Elias waits for her to choose the next field.

BIOLOGICAL CHILD — DECEASED. Age four. Helen's later pregnancy unsafe. Counselling. A lawful adoption attempt deferred over replacement concerns.

ZOE
They'd lost a daughter.

ELIAS
Yes.

Zoe opens the sponsor summary. AC019. Pre-existing relationship with the male applicant.

ZOE
Mac already knew Brian.

Withdrawal: Brian's legality objection. Four days after sponsor contact, Helen reopened the route. The promised stable, loving home would outweigh the barrier to ordinary adoption.

The disclosures are separate: existing safeguarding pathway, origin process potentially unfinished, changed records, continuing restricted administration. Both applicants accepted.

ZOE
They knew there was a line.

ELIAS
Enough to see it. Not proof they knew every altered order.

The suitability fields describe an ordinary street, work, routines, attachments. Zoe almost smiles at a memory of Brian defending a broken bathroom cabinet.

Then: FAMILIAL RESEMBLANCE DESIRED. PRIOR-CHILD ANCHOR ACTIVE.

A minimum definition: hair, face shape, crooked smile. Further detail remains folded shut.

ZOE
No more.

Elias closes it immediately.

A17-04221. MATCH SCORE 97.

Zoe turns away from the screen.

INT. HELEN AND BRIAN'S BEDROOM - SAME MORNING

Helen brings a storage box down from the loft. Brian joins her on the floor.

A photograph: their daughter laughing by a climbing frame. One small shoe lies beside it.

HELEN
She looked like her.

BRIAN
Mac didn't say it meant anything.

HELEN
He didn't have to.

Helen puts the photograph back. Not hidden under the other objects. Their daughter is still dead. Zoe is still somebody else.

EXT. FOOTPATH - AFTERNOON

Zoe kicks a stone ahead of them.

ZOE
Helen loved me because I looked like her.

ELIAS
It may explain the first feeling. It doesn't explain five years.

ZOE
That sounds like defending her.

ELIAS
It doesn't give her the right.

They walk without agreeing to make either truth smaller.

ZOE
Something horrible started something real. Then the real thing hid the horrible part.

ELIAS
Yes.

ZOE
Disgusting.

ELIAS
Yes.

ZOE
Normal agreement. Rare.

INT. GUESTHOUSE - EVENING

Zoe reads only the attachment prediction. Love made Helen and Brian vulnerable to exposure; Zoe's attachment would make questioning the beginning painful too.

She closes the household profile before P3 — CONTROLLED VISUAL INTRODUCTION.

ZOE
How did they first see me?

ELIAS
We haven't opened that.

Her paperback lies beside the computer. She takes the book, leaves the file closed for tonight.

= SC046 — Through glass

INT. GUESTHOUSE - THURSDAY MORNING

Voss's message contains a floor plan and old booking metadata. THIS MAY BE WHAT P3 MEANT.

Zoe traces two rooms with one finger. Observation glass between them.

After-hours booking. 17:30. P06-11803. RCP-0193. Sponsor present. Household present. A child-support worker on the visible side with ordinary access.

ZOE
That worker could have thought it was a normal assessment.

ELIAS
Yes.

ZOE
Did I know they were there?

ELIAS
No evidence you were told.

The plan is still a plan. The next images belong to adult recollection, not recovered surveillance.

MEMORY — WESTMERE FAMILY-ASSESSMENT CENTRE, YEARS EARLIER

Mac leads Helen and Brian into a room facing a dark pane. Brian stays beside the door.

BRIAN
You said we'd discuss a match.

MAC
We are. You can leave.

BRIAN
We should.

HELEN
Five minutes.

The other room brightens. A support worker enters with young Zoe, a paperback beneath her arm. Zoe pulls a face at the mirror. The worker laughs.

Helen's hand rises to her mouth. Brian sees the resemblance too.

BRIAN
You knew.

MAC
The resemblance was recorded.

Zoe drops a pencil, retrieves it, pushes back her hair in a movement entirely her own. Helen uses the difference as permission to keep watching.

HELEN
She looks happy.

BRIAN
You don't know that. You don't know who might be waiting for her.

Mac refuses an orphan story, refuses to claim voluntary surrender. Origin information is restricted; the safeguarding pathway is unfinished.

HELEN
Would we love her?

BRIAN
That's not the question. Do we have the right to take her?

No answer changes the room. They leave without accepting.

MEMORY — HELEN AND BRIAN'S KITCHEN, THE NEXT MORNING

Untouched tea. Helen calls Mac on speaker. Brian can still refuse.

HELEN
I want to proceed.

MAC (PHONE)
Both of you?

Helen looks at Brian. He nods.

HELEN
Yes.

INT. GUESTHOUSE - PRESENT MORNING

Zoe compares the recorded discussion summary and acceptance timing.

ZOE
They left. They had time. Then they chose.

ELIAS
Yes.

ZOE
They could love me and still not have the right.

He lets the sentence stand.

EXT. FORMER ASSESSMENT CENTRE - LATE MORNING

Now offices and vacant units. Zoe stops across the road. Does not cross the fence or photograph the entrance. Cannot identify the exact window.

Traffic passes. Someone opens a window in a working office.

ZOE
That's enough.

INT. BEIGE ESTATE - DAY

The notebook opens, then closes blank.

ZOE
I want what they did known. Mac. The match. The payments. But not every kid in that drive online because I'm angry.

ELIAS
No.

Her response to the glass remains hers. The car pulls away.

= SC047 — Three messages

INT. CONTRACTOR WORKROOM - THURSDAY AFTERNOON

An unknown number sends Voss three messages. Vale reads over his shoulder.

Tell Zoe I did not give them the second route.

They made me give the first one.

My brother and sisters are alive.

Voss does not call the number back. Preserves the image and sends it through the limited relay.

VALE
Jackie.

VOSS
Likely.

INT. BEIGE ESTATE - AFTERNOON

Elias sees the message arrive while driving. He leaves the screen untouched until the car is parked behind a farm shop. Rain ticks on the windscreen. Engine off.

He passes Zoe the image. She reads the first line. Then the next. Her grip tightens.

Outside, a woman loads potatoes into a boot. Somebody has handwritten EGGS on a board. Everything is offensively ordinary.

Zoe reads all three again.

ZOE
She's alive.

ELIAS
Likely Jackie.

She nods. Doesn't fight the word. Tears arrive before she has decided whether to let them.

The phone number offers an apparent way forward. Her thumb moves towards it, then stops.

ZOE
It sounds like her. It isn't proof of the phone.

ELIAS
No.

Zoe turns the screen face down. Picks it up again almost immediately.

The message revises what she believed, not all the facts she still lacks. One route spoken under force. Another not given. Three siblings alive. She cannot yet tell how much the sender knows about them.

ELIAS
What do you need?

ZOE
Jackie.

He stays beside her rather than turning that answer into a destination. Zoe breathes until the next words fit.

ZOE
We had a question. Nobody else would answer it wrong in the right way.

She asks for the relay, not a direct call. For this moment, a question will have to come before a rescue.

= SC048 — Jackie answers

INT. BEIGE ESTATE - FRIDAY MORNING

Zoe sends a private challenge through the relay: Purple penguin.

INT. WATCHED HOUSE - SAME MORNING

Jackie has a borrowed phone while its owner smokes outside. Cameras watch both exits; leaving could make somebody else pay.

She types: Green giraffe. Stop using animal security. It was stupid when we were nine.

Another message: Do not come here.

She puts the phone back and pours water before the owner returns. Walks upstairs at an ordinary pace.

INT. CONTRACTOR WORKROOM / BEIGE ESTATE - MORNING

Voss forwards the response, then powers down the relay device. Zoe laughs once through tears.

ZOE
She insulted the phrase. That's Jackie.

ELIAS
Much stronger. The house still isn't safe.

Zoe honours DO NOT COME HERE. Asks whether Cain still has a way to reach the siblings.

Jackie's narrow answer arrives: recent videos, a missing tooth, a changed school bag, new hair. They knew before she did.

MEMORY — JACKIE'S QUESTIONING, AFTER THE FIRST DECOY

Repeated questions. Jackie refuses. Then three children appear on a screen. The youngest has a front tooth missing.

A man says they can keep the children alive. He need not finish the threat.

Jackie gives the airport route. Has to repeat it.

INT. BEIGE ESTATE - PRESENT

The account specifies before the flight. Later, after the airport failed, they asked about the cottage and other destinations. She did not know.

ZOE
She gave the route.

ELIAS
Yes.

ZOE
Not because she chose Cain.

ELIAS
No.

The two truths hurt together. Zoe's anger finds a simpler target.

ZOE
You could kill him.

ELIAS
If Cain dies, Jackie is still inside something.

Zoe looks at the backpack. Could search the siblings. Reach them. Turn them into leverage again.

She closes the notebook before any names are entered.

ZOE
We won't use them.

ELIAS
Contact is Jackie's choice.

Zoe sends the boundary. Jackie answers: Not yet. Please. I need them out, not because I traded something else.

INT. WATCHED HOUSE - LATER

Another brief phone window. Jackie reports adults arguing about records and Westmere. The same legal woman at two places; recurring transport men. Some staff may have believed the restrictions lawful. She cannot say.

Mac hears Jackie accessed a phone, not what she sent. He calls Margaret. Still does not choose a response.

Jackie's final warning reaches the relay: when Cain learns, they will move her. She can report when, maybe not where.

INT. GUESTHOUSE - EVENING

Zoe writes SECOND DECOY. Stops before assigning anyone a task.

ZOE
Nobody gets the whole route this time.

Jackie sends: I am not wearing your coat again.

Zoe laughs properly. Replies about the sleeves. Then writes beneath the heading:

JACKIE IS NOT BAIT.

ELIAS
That stays.

# SEQ-11 — THE SECOND DECOY OPENS A REAL PROCESS

= SC049 — Partial plans

INT. ROADSIDE CAFE OUTSIDE LUDLOW - FRIDAY MORNING

Five people hold five pieces. Zoe and Elias have chips, a laminated menu and a notebook. Voss and Vale work separately. Jackie remains inside the watched house.

SECOND DECOY. JACKIE IS NOT BAIT.

ZOE
She knew the whole real route last time. One person to break.

ELIAS
Not this time.

ZOE
Including me.

She hates that more than she expected.

09:28. Jackie reports an early shift change, bags downstairs, a familiar and an unfamiliar driver. Cain absent; legal woman present. Then: shoes removed from the hall, a new coat waiting.

Zoe does not request a plate, stolen name or photograph.

INT. CONTRACTOR WORKROOM - MORNING

Voss calls one legacy standby channel. Gives AC019. No destination, no claim he has Zoe or Jackie. Asks whether it remains active. The hesitation before the answer is useful, not proof of a new job.

Elsewhere Vale enquires about one vehicle supplier seen around recent movements. Later a reservation appears: TRANSIT — 11:45. Client hidden.

She records the screen and sends: POSSIBLE MOVEMENT. NO CAUSATION.

INT. ROADSIDE CAFE - MORNING

Elias holds two fallback locations, disclosed to Zoe when needed. She checks the limited message she can author herself:

P06-11803 / SOURCE AUTHORITY CONTRADICTION CONFIRMED.
ORIGINAL PRESENTATION SITE. 13:00.

ZOE
That's my case.

ELIAS
Yes.

ZOE
No child name. No drive. No promise we're there.

He checks those facts. She sends through the bounded channel.

INT. CAIN'S OFFICE - 10:47

Restricted legal reads him the message. Cain recognises the centre. One-way glass. A history he thought finished.

He adds what the message never said: archive, witness, Zoe.

CAIN
Hold it. I'm checking.

EXT. WATCHED HOUSE - 10:58

Jackie walks out as Jackie, in the new jacket someone chose. No Zoe disguise. The legal woman carries a folder. A dark people carrier pulls away.

Jackie counts turns to give fear a job. Doesn't pretend they make a usable map.

Inside the vehicle, the legal woman gets a call about P06. Says it isn't their route. Then tells the driver to continue.

INT. FALLBACK CAR - LATER

Zoe waits with the receiver. No new message from Jackie.

ZOE
If he ignores me?

ELIAS
Her move is still happening. We don't get to invent certainty.

She closes the notebook. No last-minute demand that Jackie become more useful.

= SC050 — The safeguarding call

INT. PEOPLE CARRIER / LEGAL OFFICE YARD - FRIDAY MIDDAY

The driver's instruction changes from north to an office yard. The legal woman goes inside. Jackie waits with a driver whose knowledge she cannot assess.

A call puts the vehicle on hold. Authority must be verified before transfer.

INT. WESTMERE COMPLIANCE OFFICE - MIDDAY

Elaine sees a current query on the old presentation site. She preserves booking logs and calls North Vale. Her own decision, not instructions from Zoe.

EXT. FORMER ASSESSMENT CENTRE - 12:31

Cain arrives with one man. A standby Transit waits nearby. No Zoe. No archive. No contractors.

He looks at the empty building, then calls about Jackie.

CAIN
I ordered no hold.

The route does not give him an answer he controls.

EXT. MOTORWAY SERVICES - 12:58

The disputed move has reached public waiting. The legal woman and driver argue beside the bonnet. Jackie's rear door is unlocked.

Twenty feet to the building. A school coach empties nearby. For a moment she could disappear among other children.

She stays. Running alone would not answer the threat to her siblings. Other children will not become her disguise.

INT. MAC'S OFFICE - DAY

Mac obtains a duty safeguarding contact from Margaret. The name and number give him a way to report; no command over the response.

He tells the officer a child is being moved under disputed authority, communication restricted, held at several properties. Answers what he can. Supplies the current location and his full name.

DUTY OFFICER (PHONE)
Our decision. Our record. You won't receive a destination or staff details.

MAC
Understood.

EXT. MOTORWAY SERVICES - DAY

Following the referral, the legal administrator refuses to continue the transfer under disputed authority. She signs her decision into the current process, whatever her motive.

Two women arrive. The older opens the car door but does not pull Jackie out.

SARAH
Sarah. This is Leanne. Local authority safeguarding.

JACKIE
Prove it.

Identification. A published public-office number. Leanne calls it while Jackie listens. Verification goes outside the paperwork chain that brought her here.

JACKIE
Temporary? Can I have a solicitor?

SARAH
Yes. We need to discuss safe contact too. Not permission to take everything from you.

Jackie gets out when enough checks match. The legal woman signs a handover. Its time is recorded.

INT. FALLBACK CAR - DAY

Priya's first update is explicitly UNCONFIRMED. HANDOVER INITIATED. DO NOT MOVE.

Zoe stays put. Later Jackie describes the council identification, water and offered solicitor through the protected relay. No destination attached.

Zoe covers her mouth. Elias does not fill the silence with credit.

INT. MAC'S OFFICE - AFTERNOON

Margaret calls. The team confirmed receipt of his referral, nothing more.

MARGARET (PHONE)
You did one thing. It doesn't erase the rest.

MAC
I know.

INT. FALLBACK ROOM - EVENING

Jackie reports her siblings checked alive, locations withheld. She finally has a secret of her own.

Zoe sends: Keep it. Then: Purple penguin.

The reply comes later: Still stupid.

= SC051 — Inside and outside

INT. COUNCIL INTERVIEW ROOM - SATURDAY MORNING

Jackie has checked Nisha Patel's identity twice. Claire sits opposite with a pad.

JACKIE
Door closed. No officer from yesterday. Nobody contacts my siblings yet.

Nisha writes all three.

CLAIRE
You can stop. You don't need a complete account today.

JACKIE
If I stop, do you send me back?

Claire explains that stopping an interview is not permission to return her to danger. Nisha confirms the limit without promising choices no safeguarding team can guarantee.

Jackie describes houses, recurring men and the legal woman. A staff member once offered social-worker contact, then another restricted it.

Claire writes: POSSIBLY LEGITIMATE STAFF. KNOWLEDGE UNKNOWN.

Jackie reads the line before continuing.

JACKIE
Cain wasn't always in charge. They argued about records and moving me.

Claire leaves room for an untidy organisation.

INT. HELEN AND BRIAN'S KITCHEN - DAY

An official letter asks what they understood and whether Zoe is at risk.

BRIAN
Call Mac.

He regrets it before Helen answers.

HELEN
Did we think it was legal?

BRIAN
No.

HELEN
Then start there.

They contact an independent solicitor. Helen gives Mac's full name. Neither withdraws it.

INT. MAC'S OFFICE - DAY

Mac sends his signed sponsor-introduction agreement to Margaret. No child records or addresses. A copy, not the original. The original's location is not yet supplied.

Margaret arranges controlled transfer through separate counsel.

INT. GUESTHOUSE - AFTERNOON

Through Jackie and Priya, Zoe has been offered independent advice. No initial demand for her location.

Priya cannot represent everyone. Zoe checks several names, then Ruth Ellison. Published details checked separately; Elias calls the public office. Zoe chooses.

At the call, Ruth explains whom she represents, confidentiality and possible legal limits before asking for facts. Zoe interrupts for examples. Elias leaves when asked.

RUTH (PHONE)
What do you want now?

ZOE
My wishes in the record. Jackie safe. No whole-drive dump. No ordinary carers blamed because somebody used their system. Helen and Brian don't decide what happens to me next.

RUTH (PHONE)
What do you want from them?

Zoe looks at the blank wall.

ZOE
I don't know.

RUTH (PHONE)
That can be recorded too.

INT. COUNCIL ROOM - EVENING

Claire offers Jackie a temporary placement description. Not an acceptance already arranged for her.

JACKIE
Can I see it first?

CLAIRE
Yes.

JACKIE
Then I haven't said yes.

Claire records that exactly.

INT. PUB ROOM - EVENING

Zoe and Elias eat takeaway. Receiver off during food. Ruth now holds her wishes independently. Jackie is in a process Zoe cannot control.

Zoe steals a noodle from the wrong box. Elias looks at her.

ZOE
Independent decision.

He moves the box, leaving hers within reach.

= SC052 — Owned conduct

INT. PRIYA'S OFFICE - SUNDAY MORNING

Three controlled copies of a contractor account. Historic identifier, retained records, personal actions. No current child address or guesses disguised as fact.

Voss signs. Vale reads the final paragraph again and signs her own declaration.

PRIYA
Voluntarily? No promised immunity?

VOSS
Legacy counsel offered favourable wording if we surrendered material. We preserved the offer.

VALE
I was there. I want my part to say what I did.

Priya witnesses their signatures. No forgiveness accompanies the ink.

INT. SEPARATE INTERVIEW ROOMS - MORNING

Helen and Brian speak separately, their own solicitor available. Safeguarding interviews, no immunity and no rehearsed shared account.

HELEN
I knew the ordinary adoption process didn't apply. Her family process might not be finished.

INVESTIGATOR
Why believe your home was better?

Helen looks at her hands.

HELEN
I didn't know theirs. I called Mac. I wasn't forced.

In the other room Brian remembers the observation room as warm. Helen remembered cold. The moral facts do not depend on matching the temperature.

BRIAN
I wanted somebody else to cross the line. I could still have said no.

He admits he avoided questions that would make keeping Zoe harder.

Both separately confirm the viewing without Zoe awareness, leaving without an answer, then accepting later.

Helen begins a sentence meant for Zoe. Her solicitor stops her.

SOLICITOR
This statement isn't a message to her.

In the corridor the couple are stopped from comparing answers.

INT. INDEPENDENT SOLICITOR'S OFFICE - MIDDAY

Mac brings sponsor, payment and adult-process records. A sealed P06 envelope remains unopened pending handling arrangements.

SOLICITOR
These may expose you to criminal liability.

MAC
Her name is Zoe.

He signs authority for controlled adult-side disclosure, including the stability confirmation shortly before recovery status opened.

INT. TEMPORARY PLACEMENT - AFTERNOON

Jackie visits before choosing. Bathroom lock. Bedroom window. Staff phones stay with staff.

JACKIE
If I hate it, can I leave?

Claire explains: not detained, but thirteen and under safeguarding; walking into danger may require intervention.

JACKIE
Annoying. Honest.

She chooses the place. Asks Nisha to check her message only for location detail: I chose the boring house. I want to see Zoe when it stops being useful to everyone else.

INT. FIELD COORDINATOR'S OFFICE - AFTERNOON

Cain asks for surveillance of Helen and Brian. No contact, he says. The coordinator asks for lawful instruction. None arrives. The task is refused.

INT. GUESTHOUSE - EVENING

Ruth reports separate preservation and accounts, not every private fact. Elias remains outside the call.

Zoe permits Helen and Brian to write. Does not yet permit delivery to her.

ZOE
Facts first. Not a letter asking me to make it easier.

The backpack rests by the wall. Zoe looks at it after the call.

ZOE
Tomorrow we stop testing.

Elias waits for the rest, but that is all.

= SC053 — Irreversible

INT. GUESTHOUSE - MONDAY MORNING

Ruth appears on the laptop. Zoe's notebook faces her; Elias sits beyond the camera.

ZOE
Detonation.

RUTH
Controlled disclosure in anything I write.

Zoe has three rules: no whole-drive dump, no unrelated children used to prove adult conduct, no sole copy of the truth.

Ruth qualifies them honestly. Jackie own information may be necessary to investigate Jackie. Police may lawfully require relevant records. Zoe cannot decide every other child's interests.

Zoe acknowledges each limit. Her original drive and personal folder remain closed.

Ruth identifies separate recipients and scope: institutions for their authority records; investigators for contractor/current-coercion and verification evidence; counsel for adult sponsor and payment material.

Journalist Daniel Rusk receives less: redacted contradiction, chronology, authenticated adult sponsor link and questions he can check independently. No girls' names, no statements or child files.

DANIEL (SPEAKER)
This is thin.

ZOE
It's supposed to be. Check it.

INT. DANIEL'S WORKSPACE - MORNING

Daniel calls published institutional contacts. North Vale confirms the real base reference, unsupported supplemental powers. Westmere confirms a restricted subset under review, not all placements. Priya confirms signed contractor accounts, not knowing use of forged orders. Mac's counsel authenticates AC019 and the signature, not every referral as criminal.

His draft includes WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED. No total victim count. No claim all Westmere work was unlawful.

INT. GUESTHOUSE - 10:00

RUTH
Ready?

ZOE
No whole archive. No Jackie to the press.

RUTH
Agreed.

ZOE
Do it.

Acknowledgements arrive from different recipients. Nothing explodes. Zoe switches off the receiver.

INT. INTERVIEW SUITES / LEGAL COMPANY - DAY

Voss and Vale give separate accounts consistent in their essential facts. Neither receives a deal for good faith.

Verification templates and relevant logs are preserved through forensic handling. The legal administrator who refused Cain signs a handover.

Mac's voluntary police interview changes status. He is cautioned; counsel checks whether he still intends to cooperate. He does.

EXT. A49 LAY-BY - AFTERNOON

Cain's new solicitor asks authority to share his location. Cain agrees. Later officers arrest him on suspicion of offences connected with Jackie and related matters. Hands visible. No fight, no verdict.

INT. GUESTHOUSE - LATE AFTERNOON

The independently checked article remains narrow. Zoe asks what happens if a reviewed case proves normal.

RUTH
It should be recorded as normal.

Publication risks remain; the investigations would continue if they waited. Zoe accepts the limited draft after those answers. Other outlets begin their own questions.

Ruth reads one sentence from Helen only after Zoe agrees: later care did not make the original choice theirs to make.

ZOE
Stop there.

Ruth stops.

INT. GUESTHOUSE - EVENING

Zoe crosses out DETONATION. Writes IRREVERSIBLE.

She takes out the drive and her numbered folder. They are not the same thing. Negotiates with Ruth: protected evidential handover for the original, separate solicitor custody for her folder, each with its own receipt and lawful limits.

She seals the folder in an envelope. Writes ZOE across the flap.

ZOE
I don't want to carry that drive anymore.

ELIAS
You don't have to carry it to keep it true.

Tomorrow's bag is zipped closed.

# SEQ-12 — DIRECTION

= SC054 — Custody

INT. SECURE EVIDENCE OFFICE - TUESDAY MORNING

A table, a handover form, Ruth and an evidence officer. Elias waits out of the transaction. Zoe puts the envelope down first.

ZOE across the flap.

Ruth checks the unbroken seal.

RUTH
Separately with me. Under your instructions, subject to the legal limits we discussed.

ZOE
Not with the drive. If anything changes?

RUTH
I'll explain what changes and why, as far as I'm lawfully able.

A separate receipt goes to Zoe. The envelope moves into Ruth custody, not into the officer's evidence bag.

Zoe removes the archive hard drive from the backpack. Sets it on the table herself.

The officer checks the documented item, handling conditions and original-preservation record. Relevant access to restricted child information will be recorded. Previously preserved copies remain distinct.

EVIDENCE OFFICER
Read the form first. Ask before you sign.

Zoe reads it. Checks one item against Ruth explanation. The officer waits. No speech about being brave.

She signs. Her handwriting looks oddly small.

The officer bags the original, seals it and marks the time: 09:42. The custody reference goes on Zoe's receipt. The officer, not Zoe, is now responsible for its protected handling.

The scuffed black backpack lies flat on the table. Elias repaired strap is still visible. It is suddenly only a bag.

EVIDENCE OFFICER
Do you want that back?

ZOE
Yes.

She puts the receipts in her coat with the notebook. The backpack goes over one shoulder. Its familiar weight is absent.

At the door she looks back once. Ruth has the envelope. The officer has the sealed drive. Nobody has taken the notebook.

EXT. SECURE OFFICE - MORNING

Elias joins her outside.

He notices the backpack, then waits for Zoe to speak.

ZOE
It's light.

ELIAS
Yes.

For once there is no need to explain the obvious. They walk towards the car. Her next meeting is with her friend, not with another file.

= SC055 — Jackie and Zoe

INT. CAFE BESIDE A PARK - TUESDAY NOON

Jackie chose the café. Claire sits at another table. Elias waits outside. No investigator, camera or statement hidden inside a reunion.

12:06. Jackie enters in a green coat. Zoe stands.

JACKIE
If you run at me, I leave.

Zoe stops three feet away.

JACKIE
You can hug me.

They hold each other harder than planned. No roomful of applause. When they part, Jackie wipes her face first. Neither intends to concede that detail later.

JACKIE
Your hair is awful.

ZOE
So is yours.

JACKIE
Professionals.

ZOE
Criminal professionals?

JACKIE
Probably not.

They sit. Zoe's rehearsed speech abandons her.

ZOE
I'm sorry. I decided what you'd done before I knew.

JACKIE
I thought you kept choosing them. I hated you.

ZOE
I know.

Jackie turns a sugar packet between her fingers.

JACKIE
You didn't know.

ZOE
That doesn't make it nice. I'm still sorry.

Jackie nods once. Enough to keep talking. Pours sugar into an empty cup.

ZOE
You don't have a drink.

JACKIE
Preparing.

The waiter arrives. Jackie orders chips. Zoe judges silently, very visibly.

Later Jackie offers only what she chooses about the siblings. Her brother grew. One sister draws horses badly. The youngest still hates peas. Twelve minutes of video with her brother, four spent pretending the connection failed.

No address or placement detail.

JACKIE
I don't know if we'll live together. Contact first, maybe. If they're safe, maybe they stay.

ZOE
You don't need to know today.

JACKIE
Who are you?

ZOE
Shut up.

Jackie stares towards the park.

JACKIE
I don't want to be their mother again. I was six.

Zoe does not fill the silence with a promise to fix it.

JACKIE
I want to be their sister.

ZOE
You should get to.

Jackie looks back. Nods. Zoe steals a chip; Jackie slaps her hand.

At the other table Claire looks up at the ordinary noise, then leaves them to their conversation. This part of their friendship is not evidence somebody else needs to collect.

= SC056 — A life still being decided

INT. RUTH'S OFFICE / VARIOUS - LATER THAT WEEK

Ruth gives Zoe a limited update, not a list she must memorise.

Cain is charged first over Jackie detention, movement and interference with safeguarding. Mac is charged later over placement records, conspiracy and financial arrangements; he continues to cooperate. Voss and Vale remain under investigation. Their accounts establish their actions too.

These are charges and inquiries, not convictions or a solved archive.

Westmere confirms some restricted cases legitimate. Others need work. North Vale checks further references. No final total appears.

INT. RUTH'S OFFICE - ANOTHER AFTERNOON

Helen and Brian remain in their house. Zoe watches rain on the window.

ZOE
My room?

RUTH
As far as I know, still there. They've written separately. Asked whether you might consider contact. They'll accept no.

Zoe remembers a badly repaired cabinet, school mornings, Helen untangling hair. Those memories stand alongside the glass.

ZOE
Not yet. Don't destroy the letters.

Three days later she asks for Helen first page only. Reads a handwritten admission: she knew enough to refuse; loss and grief explain her, not a right to choose for Zoe or her original family.

Zoe folds the page.

ZOE
Enough.

Ruth takes it back. Brian envelope remains unopened.

INT. TEMPORARY ACCOMMODATION - DAYS LATER

The location and practical rules were explained through Ruth and current safeguarding. Nothing claims permanence. Elias has a separate room down the corridor.

He returns from a doctor and tells Zoe himself. Rib pain, previous physical dependence and fear of repeating it discussed. The pain plan changed. No miraculous recovery.

Zoe does not count his tablets or become his monitor. He takes prescribed treatment without making refusal a test of strength.

INT. RUTH'S OFFICE - TWO WEEKS AFTER THE ARTICLE

RUTH
What name do you want used?

ZOE
Mine.

RUTH
Which one?

The earlier name is in the sealed folder. Zoe does not need to erase either history to choose today.

ZOE
Use Zoe. Keep the other one private until I decide otherwise.

Ruth explains records may eventually require correction; not everything must be settled now. Writes Zoe wishes, not an adult substitute.

INT. TEMPORARY ACCOMMODATION - LATER

Jackie calls about her new school: assigned resilience, refused, received coastal erosion instead. Zoe laughs until she has to call back.

Her own tutor has left algebra on the table. Surviving a conspiracy has not improved it.

The news article moves off the front page. The investigations do not stop. Zoe puts the phone down and returns to one difficult equation, with a future not reducible to the archive.

= SC057 — The ridge

EXT. PARK BENCH - AFTERNOON

Zoe and Jackie watch a dog trying to drag a stick through a fence. Claire is nearby, outside their conversation.

JACKIE
Do you forgive them?

ZOE
I don't know.

Jackie accepts that answer. The dog abandons the stick, chases something else.

JACKIE
Getting away should have felt bigger.

ZOE
Maybe that's not the ending.

Jackie waits.

ZOE
Agency.

JACKIE
Homework.

ZOE
Choice.

JACKIE
Say choice then.

They watch the dog return empty-mouthed. Neither girl needs a final verdict today.

INT. ELIAS'S ROOM - EVENING

His bag is partly packed. Zoe stands in the doorway. He talks about distance, protecting her by leaving.

ZOE
If you want to go, say that. Don't decide what I want and call it protection.

He sits. She takes the chair opposite. He can still choose to leave; she has not ordered him to stay.

ELIAS
I don't know how to stay without being responsible for everything.

ZOE
Then don't be responsible for everything.

ELIAS
You're thirteen. There'll be danger. Times I know more.

ZOE
You say move now. I move. Afterward, you explain. The rest we work out.

He takes time before answering her actual question.

ELIAS
I don't want to leave.

ZOE
Then unpack.

ELIAS
Bossy.

ZOE
Agency.

He laughs. Unpacks two shirts. Not everything. Enough for now.

INT. BREAKFAST ROOM - SATURDAY MORNING

A message from Ruth: next hearing, another reference for review, nothing Zoe must do today. She turns off the phone.

Into the backpack: water, crisps, fantasy book, phone. No folder. No drive.

ELIAS
Walk?

ZOE
Why?

ELIAS
Because.

Suspicious. Acceptable. She picks up the bag.

EXT. PATH JUNCTION - SATURDAY

After nearly a mile beside a hedge, the path divides. Left climbs towards the ridge. Right drops towards a river.

Elias stops with Zoe.

ELIAS
Which way?

ZOE
What do you think?

ELIAS
River. Less uphill.

ZOE
Strong argument. I want the view.

ELIAS
Then ridge.

ZOE
You said river.

ELIAS
You asked what I thought. Now you decide.

ZOE
No. We decide.

He looks at both paths again.

ELIAS
Ridge first. River on the way back.

She considers the proposal.

ZOE
Acceptable.

They turn left together. The backpack hangs loose from one shoulder.
